// AINOW Agriculture & AgriTech Library - 100 Unique Prompts
export const agriculturePrompts = [
  {"id":"agr-1","title":"Corn Hybrid Selection for High-Yield DeKalb County","prompt":"You are an agronomist advising a 450-acre family corn-soybean operation in central Illinois (DeKalb County, USDA Zone 5b) with predominantly Drummer silty clay loam soils (CEC 22-28 meq/100g, OM 4.2%). The farm averages 210 bu/acre corn under conventional management. Evaluate five Pioneer and DEKALB hybrids rated 108–112 days relative maturity for this geography, comparing disease packages (gray leaf spot, northern corn leaf blight), standability scores, and documented yield trial data from the Illinois Crop Sciences university trials. Produce a side-by-side comparison table, a recommended planting population by hybrid (seeds/acre), and a seeding date window based on GDD accumulation targets for a May 1–10 planting. Conclude with your top two hybrid selections and the agronomic rationale for each.","tags":["crop-production","agronomy","corn","hybrid-selection","yield"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"agr-2","title":"Soybean Population and Row Spacing Optimization","prompt":"You are a university extension agronomist specializing in soybean production systems for the eastern Corn Belt (Ohio, Indiana, Michigan). A 320-acre operation in Wayne County, Ohio (predominantly Wooster silt loam, pH 6.4, OM 2.8%) is asking whether switching from 30-inch rows at 140,000 seeds/acre to 15-inch rows at 120,000 seeds/acre is economically justified given current equipment costs. Analyze the yield response data from Ohio State University soybean population trials (2019–2023), model the ROI at $12.50/bu soybeans, and factor in the $18,000 equipment modification cost amortized over 5 years. Produce a break-even analysis table, a recommendation on row spacing and final stand targets, and a planting date recommendation tied to soil temperature thresholds (50°F at 2-inch depth). Provide specific management adjustments for SCN-susceptible fields (Heterodera glycines HG type 2.5.7).","tags":["crop-production","soybean","row-spacing","yield-optimization","economics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-3","title":"Winter Wheat Fertility Program for Mid-Atlantic Dryland Fields","prompt":"You are a certified crop adviser (CCA) working with a 600-acre dryland winter wheat operation in the Virginia Shenandoah Valley (Page County, Berks channery silt loam, pH 6.1, P-Mehlich3 28 ppm, K 142 ppm, OM 2.1%). The grower achieved 68 bu/acre last season and targets 80 bu/acre this cycle. Design a complete fertility program from pre-plant through Feekes 10.5 flag-leaf application, specifying: (1) fall pre-plant 18-46-0 DAP rate in lb/acre based on soil test sufficiency levels, (2) spring topdress urea timing and rate split between Feekes 4 and Feekes 6 using a 1.3 lb N/bu yield goal, (3) sulfur supplementation as AMS at 15 lb S/acre if OM is below 2.5%, and (4) a fungicide decision rule for Fusarium head blight based on the Fusarium Risk Tool forecast probability threshold of 50%. Present the full program as a dated agronomic calendar with product, rate, timing, and application method for each input.","tags":["crop-production","wheat","fertility","nitrogen-management","fungicide"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-4","title":"Cotton Defoliation Timing Decision Protocol","prompt":"You are an agronomist advising a 1,200-acre upland cotton operation in the Mississippi Delta (Bolivar County, Sharkey clay, USDA Zone 8a). The grower targets a 1,200 lb/acre lint yield and uses CROPLAN 3527B3XF and PhytoGen PHY 340 W3FE varieties. Develop a defoliation timing decision protocol that integrates: (1) node above cracked boll (NACB) threshold of 4 nodes or fewer, (2) open boll percentage target of 60%, (3) four-week frost-free window verification using 10-year NOAA climate normals for September–October, and (4) product selection matrix comparing thidiazuron + ethephon versus tribufos (DEF) + ethephon at two harvest aid rates. Include a regrowth (re-greening) contingency plan if a second defoliation is needed within 10 days, and outline the economic consequence of each 1-day delay in harvest on fiber quality (color grade, micronaire, staple length).","tags":["crop-production","cotton","defoliation","harvest-management","Delta"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-5","title":"Corn Yield Drag Diagnosis from Compaction Layers","prompt":"You are a certified crop adviser investigating unexplained 25–30 bu/acre corn yield drag on specific field zones in a 780-acre operation in Kossuth County, Iowa (Canisteo silty clay loam). GPS yield maps show consistent underperformance in areas that received manure application in wet springs (2019, 2021). Design a field investigation protocol that includes: (1) penetrometer readings at 2-inch increments to 18 inches to identify tillage pan depth, with action threshold of 300 psi, (2) soil bulk density sampling at 6-inch and 12-inch depths compared to critical threshold of 1.55 g/cm³ for silty clay loam, (3) root dig evaluation at V6 and VT stages documenting root mass, root tip color, and lateral root restriction, and (4) deep-zone tillage (Salford RTS or similar) economic ROI calculation at $35/acre operation cost versus projected $40/acre yield recovery value at $4.75/bu corn. Produce a zone-specific remediation map and a 3-year monitoring plan.","tags":["crop-production","compaction","yield-diagnosis","soil-science","Iowa"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-6","title":"Dryland Sorghum Production Calendar for the Texas Panhandle","prompt":"You are a Texas A&M AgriLife extension agronomist advising a 2,400-acre dryland grain sorghum operation in Castro County, Texas (Pullman clay loam, pH 7.8, OM 1.2%, average annual precipitation 18 inches). The grower is converting 800 acres from failed dryland cotton. Build a complete production calendar from land preparation through harvest for a target yield of 4,500 lb/acre, including: (1) pre-plant soil nitrate-N sampling to 24 inches and sidedress UAN-32 rate calculation at 1.1 lb N per 100 lb grain yield goal, (2) hybrid selection criteria (stay-green trait, head bug and MDMV tolerance) from Pioneer and Advanta trial data, (3) planting date window tied to 65°F soil temperature at 2-inch depth and 10-day forecast, (4) hegari midge scouting protocol with 10% bloom threshold for pyrethroids, and (5) pre-harvest desiccation decision based on 25% grain moisture for combine harvest. Format as a month-by-month action calendar with decision triggers and input costs per acre.","tags":["crop-production","sorghum","dryland","Texas","crop-calendar"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-7","title":"High-Tunnel Tomato Production System for Direct-Market Growers","prompt":"You are a vegetable systems specialist with Cornell Cooperative Extension advising a 3-acre direct-market vegetable farm in the Hudson Valley, New York (Ulster County, Wayland silt loam, pH 6.5, P 45 ppm Mehlich-3). The grower wants to install two 30x96-foot gothic-arch high tunnels to extend the tomato season from 12 to 22 weeks and grow indeterminate heirloom varieties (Brandywine, Cherokee Purple) alongside F1 beefsteak types. Design the complete production system including: (1) caterpillar/low tunnel priming schedule to establish transplants 6 weeks before last frost (May 5 average), (2) fertigation program using drip tape at 12-inch emitter spacing with Peters Professional 20-20-20 and calcium-nitrate weekly at EC 2.5–3.0 mS/cm, (3) pruning and training protocol on single-leader Dutch-style system with weekly clipping to vertical twine, (4) Botrytis and early blight integrated management using copper octanoate plus Serenade at 7-day intervals with ventilation targets of 80°F maximum, and (5) pricing strategy for CSA and farmers market channels using cost-of-production data at $1.85/lb break-even. Deliver a weekly production schedule from transplanting through final harvest.","tags":["crop-production","high-tunnel","tomato","direct-market","Northeast"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-8","title":"Alfalfa Stand Establishment and Persistence Plan","prompt":"You are a forage agronomist advising a 480-acre dairy operation in Dane County, Wisconsin (Fayette silt loam, pH 6.8, K 185 ppm, B 0.4 ppm Mehlich-3) that is planting 120 acres of alfalfa to replace aging 6-year-old stands averaging 2.8 tons DM/acre. Develop a complete stand establishment plan that covers: (1) seedbed preparation comparing conventional tillage versus no-till interseeding into small grain stubble with corresponding yield timeline differences, (2) variety selection for winterhardiness index 2.0 or lower (WI Zone 4) with high multi-leaf and Aphanomyces Race 2 resistance from the National Alfalfa Alliance trial data, (3) seeding rate of 15 lb/acre with inoculant (Rhizobium meliloti USDA 1002 strain) application protocol, (4) first cutting decision based on 10% bloom for stand persistence versus 60-day rest period in year one, and (5) boron and potassium maintenance program at K removal rate of 50 lb K₂O per ton DM. Present a 5-year yield projection and replacement cost analysis.","tags":["crop-production","alfalfa","forage","stand-establishment","dairy-systems"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-9","title":"Rice Water Management Protocol for Gulf Coast Paddy Systems","prompt":"You are a rice specialist with the LSU AgCenter advising a 1,800-acre commercial rice operation in Acadia Parish, Louisiana (Crowley fine sandy loam, pH 6.2, OM 1.8%) growing Clearfield CL111 and PVL01 long-grain varieties. Develop a season-long water management protocol integrating: (1) flood establishment timing — pinpoint flood at 4-leaf stage (21 DAS) to 4-inch depth to suppress barnyardgrass and sprangletop, (2) permanent flood depth management at 4–6 inches through internode elongation, (3) delayed flood management for straighthead disorder prevention on susceptible fields with >3 ppm soil arsenic, (4) midseason drain window (7–10 days at PI) for nitrogen sidedress application of 50 lb N/acre as urea, (5) pre-harvest drain schedule targeting 100 GDD before combine harvest at 22% grain moisture. Calculate water usage in acre-inches per season and the pumping cost per cwt at $0.12/kWh. Identify the three most economically impactful water management errors and how to avoid them.","tags":["crop-production","rice","water-management","Louisiana","paddy-systems"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-10","title":"Dry Bean Production Optimization for Northern Plains Direct Sales","prompt":"You are an agronomist and market specialist advising a 300-acre diversified grain farm in Stutsman County, North Dakota (Barnes loam, pH 7.1, OM 3.4%) adding dry beans (navy and black beans) as a specialty crop for direct sales and local food market contracts at $0.45/lb. Design a complete production and marketing plan that includes: (1) field selection criteria — well-drained soils, no previous white mold history, proper field shape for combine harvest, (2) variety selection from NDSU Bean Project trials targeting 2,200 lb/acre navy and 2,000 lb/acre black bean yields, (3) fertility program capping N at 80 lb/acre (beans fix 60–80% of N needs with Brady rhizobium inoculant at planting), (4) white mold scouting protocol and Endura (boscalid) application at 10% bloom with 80% canopy closure threshold, (5) vine desiccation with paraquat at 90% pod color change for uniform harvest, and (6) direct-market value calculation: 2,000 lb/acre × $0.45/lb = $900/acre gross versus $380/acre commodity at $9.50/cwt. Produce a break-even price and minimum contract volume analysis.","tags":["crop-production","dry-beans","specialty-crops","North-Dakota","direct-market"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-11","title":"Soil Health Transition Plan Using Cover Crops and Reduced Tillage","prompt":"You are a NRCS soil health specialist working with a 520-acre grain farm in Champaign County, Illinois (Flanagan silty clay loam, CEC 24 meq/100g, OM 3.1%, Haney test biological activity score 8.2) transitioning from fall chisel plow to no-till over three years. [Paste your soil test results] Design the transition plan specifying: (1) Year 1 cover crop mix — cereal rye (60 lb/acre) + hairy vetch (10 lb/acre) seeded at corn harvest into standing residue via high-boy at V6, terminated with roller-crimper at vetch 50% bloom to add 80–100 lb N/acre through biomass decomposition, (2) Year 2 addition of radish (5 lb/acre) to the mix for biopore creation to 18-inch depth, targeting bulk density reduction from 1.52 to 1.38 g/cm³ by Year 3, (3) year-by-year soil health metric targets: OM increase of 0.1% per year, WFPS 55–65% in rooting zone, aggregate stability >60% by Yoder wet-sieve method, (4) NRCS EQIP cover crop payment eligibility (CP1 through CP4 practice codes) and projected cost-share at $38/acre. Produce a 3-year action calendar with measurement checkpoints.","tags":["soil-health","cover-crops","no-till","NRCS","OM-building"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-12","title":"Comprehensive Compost Program for Market Garden Fertility","prompt":"You are a soil fertility specialist advising a 15-acre certified organic market garden in Lancaster County, Pennsylvania (Hagerstown silt loam, pH 6.6, P 210 ppm Mehlich-3 — elevated, K 180 ppm, OM 4.8%) that has over-applied compost for 8 years. The high phosphorus is triggering runoff concerns and limiting further P additions under PA Clean Streams Act nutrient management. Design a phosphorus drawdown and soil health maintenance program: (1) P balance calculation — current vegetable crop P removal at 30–80 lb P₂O₅/acre/year, years to drawdown to 150 ppm target, (2) shift to compost tea and vermicompost (low-P biostimulant) at 50 gallons/acre 3x per season for microbial inoculant value without P loading, (3) cover crop mix selection emphasizing nitrogen fixation (crimson clover 15 lb + phacelia 6 lb + buckwheat 40 lb) rather than fertility inputs, (4) potassium management using sulfate of potash (0-0-50-17S) at 100 lb K₂O/acre where K falls below 150 ppm, and (5) annual Haney soil health scoring protocol comparing biological active C (POXC) baseline 680 mg/kg to 5-year target of 900 mg/kg. Produce a nutrient management plan summary table.","tags":["soil-health","compost","phosphorus-management","organic","market-garden"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-13","title":"Biochar Application Protocol for Sandy Coastal Plain Soils","prompt":"You are a soil scientist advising a 200-acre vegetable and small grain farm on the Delmarva Peninsula (Sussex County, Delaware — Sassafras fine sandy loam, pH 5.8, CEC 6 meq/100g, OM 0.9%, AWHC 0.9 in/ft). The farm manager wants to use wood biochar to improve nutrient and water retention in these highly leachable soils before planting processing sweet corn under a Del Monte contract. Design a biochar application and soil amendment protocol: (1) biochar sourcing — hardwood pyrolyzed at 600°C minimum, particle size 1–6mm, pH 8.5–9.5, ash content <10%, BET surface area >300 m²/g, (2) co-application with compost at 2:1 compost-to-biochar ratio to pre-charge biochar surface sites and prevent nutrient immobilization, (3) application rate of 2 tons biochar/acre incorporated to 8-inch depth via subsoil ripper pass, projected CEC increase to 9–11 meq/100g and AWHC to 1.3 in/ft within 2 growing seasons, (4) liming adjustment — biochar alkalinity offsets 0.5 tons lime equivalent per ton biochar applied, (5) 3-year yield monitoring protocol for sweet corn ears/acre and nitrogen use efficiency (NUE) comparison versus control strips. Include biochar cost ($480/ton) ROI analysis at sweet corn contract price of $85/ton fresh weight.","tags":["soil-health","biochar","sandy-soils","Delmarva","CEC-improvement"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-14","title":"Soil Carbon Sequestration Baseline and Monitoring Protocol","prompt":"You are an agronomist and carbon program specialist working with a 1,600-acre row crop farm in Boone County, Missouri (Mexico silt loam, OM 2.6%, SOC 1.51%) enrolling in Indigo Ag's carbon program. The farm operator wants to establish a credible soil organic carbon (SOC) baseline and design a 5-year sequestration plan. Develop the protocol: (1) stratified random sampling design — 24 sampling points at 0–12 inch and 12–24 inch depths covering 3 management zones (high/medium/low yield from EC mapping), analyzed by dry combustion LECO method with LOI correction, (2) baseline SOC calculation in Mg C/ha and projected sequestration rate of 0.3–0.5 Mg C/ha/year from continuous no-till + cover crops, (3) additionality documentation requirements: no-till history <3 years, cover crops new in enrollment year, (4) permanence risk management: 10% buffer pool deduction per Verra VM0042 methodology, (5) carbon credit generation estimate — 480 acres × 0.4 Mg C/ha × 3.67 CO₂e conversion = 703 Mg CO₂e/year at $25/tonne = $17,575/year revenue. Produce a sampling map, monitoring schedule, and net revenue analysis after program fees (15% of credits).","tags":["soil-health","carbon-sequestration","carbon-markets","monitoring-protocol","SOC"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-15","title":"Manure Nutrient Management Plan for 500-Cow Dairy","prompt":"You are a certified nutrient management planner (CNMP) developing the annual plan for a 500-cow Holstein dairy in Vilas County, Wisconsin (Plainfield loamy sand, CEC 5 meq/100g, pH 6.3, P-Bray 38 ppm, OM 1.4%) that generates 5.5 million gallons of liquid dairy manure annually at 8% DM. Wisconsin NR 243 requires a complete nutrient budget. [Paste your soil test results] Design the CNMP covering: (1) manure nutrient analysis — typical values for WI liquid dairy manure: 25 lb total N/1,000 gal (55% ammonium-N), 10 lb P₂O₅, 22 lb K₂O per 1,000 gal, (2) application rate calculation for corn silage fields at 150 lb plant-available N/acre target, accounting for first-year N availability factor of 0.75 for spring injection, (3) setback distances: 200 ft from streams, 1,000 ft from wells, 300 ft from residences under NR 151, (4) application method comparison — surface broadcast (40% volatilization loss) versus drag-hose injection (8% loss) with cost and RUSLE2 erosion rate impact, (5) P-Index calculation for high-P fields triggering zero-manure application zones. Produce a field-by-field application schedule and 5-year P drawdown projection.","tags":["soil-health","manure-management","dairy","nutrient-planning","Wisconsin"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-16","title":"Cover Crop Termination Decision Matrix for No-Till Corn","prompt":"You are an agronomist advising a network of 12 no-till corn-soybean farmers in western Indiana (Fountain and Warren counties, Ockley silt loam and Crosby silt loam mix, avg OM 2.9%) who established cereal rye cover crops at 60 lb/acre seeded September 28. It is now April 20 and the cereal rye is at Feekes 6 (first node palpable) with 18-inch average height and estimated 2,400 lb DM/acre biomass. Build a termination decision matrix weighing: (1) termination timing options — burn-down 3 weeks before corn planting (optimal: 21 days) versus roller-crimper at anthesis (6 weeks away, high allelopathy risk), (2) herbicide program — Roundup PowerMAX 32 oz/acre + Sharpen 1 oz/acre + MSO 1% v/v for full burndown, with re-entry interval and plant-back restrictions for corn, (3) nitrogen tie-up risk calculation: 2,400 lb DM × 1.6% N = 38 lb N immobilized for 4–6 weeks in residue decomposition — adjust starter N from 10 gal 10-34-0 to 15 gal to compensate, (4) soil moisture impact: rye transpiring 0.25 in/day — early termination if soil moisture below 65% field capacity at planting, (5) decision tree for weather delays: if termination window closes due to rain, switch to spring roller-crimper + post-emerge herbicide in corn at V2. Format as a laminated field decision card with yes/no branch logic.","tags":["soil-health","cover-crops","termination","no-till","nitrogen-management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-17","title":"Vermicomposting System Design for On-Farm Organic Waste","prompt":"You are a sustainable agriculture specialist advising a 75-acre diversified vegetable and pastured poultry farm in Buncombe County, North Carolina (Madison loam, pH 5.9, OM 3.2%) that generates 800 lb/week of mixed crop residue, spent chicken bedding (pine shavings + manure), and kitchen scraps from an on-site farm store. Design a continuous-flow vermicomposting system: (1) species selection — Eisenia fetida (red wigglers) at 2 lb worms per 1 lb daily food input, (2) windrow bin sizing: 4-foot-wide × 24-foot-long raised beds in a dedicated 30×60-foot covered structure with concrete floor and leachate capture drains, (3) feedstock C:N ratio management — chicken litter at C:N 10:1 blended with carbon-rich shavings and straw to target C:N 25:1 — weekly addition of 200 lb feedstock per bed, (4) temperature management: vermicompost active zone 55–77°F; install shade cloth for summer cooling and low-tunnel row cover for winter operation, (5) finished product quality targets — worm castings with OM 40–50%, CEC 40–60 meq/100g, humic acid 2–5%, and market value assessment at $0.35/lb bagged for retail versus $180/ton bulk for wholesale. Include a 12-month production ramp-up schedule and break-even analysis.","tags":["soil-health","vermicomposting","on-farm-waste","organic-amendments","diversified-farm"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-18","title":"Acidic Soil Liming Program for Appalachian Row Crops","prompt":"You are an extension soil fertility specialist advising a 380-acre corn-hay operation in Grayson County, Virginia (Clifton silt loam, current pH 5.1 — severely acidic, Al saturation 42%, Ca saturation 48%, Mg saturation 6%, OM 3.8%). Yield drag from aluminum toxicity is estimated at 35 bu/acre corn and 1.2 tons/acre mixed hay. [Paste your soil test results] Design a liming program to reach pH 6.5 within 24 months: (1) lime rate calculation — buffer pH 5.8 requires 4.2 tons ECCE 100 limestone per acre; with available dolomitic limestone at 85 ECCE, apply 4.9 tons/acre, (2) split application strategy: 3 tons/acre fall incorporation (disk 6 inches) + 2 tons/acre surface application following spring oat seeding to minimize soil disturbance on erodible slopes (RUSLE2 LS factor 4.2), (3) calcium and magnesium ratio correction — target Ca:Mg 6.5:1 using 80% calcitic + 20% dolomitic lime blend, (4) aluminum detoxification timeline: pH increase of 0.3 units per ton ECCE on these soils, Al saturation drops below 5% at pH 5.8 — expect 14-month window before full corn yield recovery, (5) follow-up micronutrient reassessment for B, Zn, and Mn which become limiting above pH 6.8. Produce a year-by-year pH and yield projection with liming cost ROI.","tags":["soil-health","liming","soil-acidity","aluminum-toxicity","Appalachian"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-19","title":"Precision Soil Sampling Grid Design for Variable-Rate Fertility","prompt":"You are a precision agriculture agronomist designing a soil sampling and variable-rate fertility program for a 960-acre corn-soybean-wheat rotation farm in Tippecanoe County, Indiana (Crosby-Brookston complex, CEC ranging 12–26 meq/100g across fields). The farm uses a John Deere Operations Center and a Case IH AFS display for VRT controller. Design the complete system: (1) EC mapping protocol using Veris MSP sensor at 2.5-mph travel speed, creating 1-acre grid EC maps correlated to CEC, clay content, and OM zones — define 3–5 management zones per field, (2) soil sampling design — zone-based composite sampling with 8 cores per zone, 0–6 inch depth for P, K, pH, and 0–24 inch for NO₃-N, analyzed by Midwest Laboratories using Bray P1, ammonium acetate K, and buffer pH methods, (3) VRT prescription generation — P₂O₅ and K₂O rates by zone using Iowa State sufficiency model: zone 1 (CEC 22, target P 35 ppm) = 80 lb P₂O₅/acre, zone 4 (CEC 13, target P 25 ppm) = 50 lb P₂O₅/acre, (4) application equipment calibration: Case IH 3230 air seeder with two-bin VRT, minimum controller response time 2 seconds at 6 mph = 18-foot reaction distance — account for in prescription boundary buffers, (5) economic validation: VRT vs. uniform rate — Iowa State trial data shows $12–18/acre net benefit on variable fields. Produce a sampling protocol document and a VRT prescription workflow.","tags":["soil-health","precision-ag","variable-rate","soil-sampling","EC-mapping"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-20","title":"Mycorrhizal Inoculant Evaluation Protocol for Organic Corn","prompt":"You are a soil biologist and agronomist advising a 220-acre certified organic grain operation in Linn County, Iowa (Tama silty clay loam, pH 6.7, OM 4.1%, Haney score 14.8, POXC 820 mg/kg) exploring mycorrhizal fungi inoculants to reduce organic-approved phosphorus inputs (rock phosphate at $460/ton). Design a rigorous on-farm trial protocol: (1) product selection — compare three commercial AM fungi products: MycoApply Ultrafine Endo (Rhizophagus irregularis), Vault HP (Bacillus + Glomus intraradices), and a no-inoculant control, applied in-furrow at planting using a liquid Kinze planter system at 1.2 fl oz/1,000 seeds, (2) trial design — 3 replications × 3 treatments = 9 strips of 8 rows × 0.5 mile each, randomized complete block, (3) assessment metrics: root colonization percentage at V6 (staining protocol with trypan blue, 50-root subsample), P uptake efficiency (tissue test at V8 targeting 0.30–0.35% P in leaf), and final yield at harvest calibrated weigh wagon, (4) soil phosphatase activity measured at R1 using fluorometric MUB-phosphate substrate assay as a proxy for mycorrhizal function, (5) economic threshold: if inoculant adds 4 bu/acre yield ($26 value) against $9/acre inoculant cost, continue; if not, redirect budget to foliar soft-rock phosphate at $18/acre. Deliver a trial report template and 3-year product performance tracking spreadsheet.","tags":["soil-health","mycorrhizal","organic-farming","biological-inputs","on-farm-trial"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-21","title":"Soybean Sudden Death Syndrome IPM Protocol","prompt":"You are a plant pathologist advising a 700-acre soybean operation in Muscatine County, Iowa (Muscatine silt loam, tile-drained, pH 6.6, OM 3.5%) that experienced 18% yield loss in 2023 from Fusarium virguliforme (SDS) in wet, cool May conditions. [Paste your crop scouting report] Design a comprehensive SDS IPM program: (1) field risk mapping using SDS risk index inputs — planting date before May 10, soil temperature <55°F at planting, SCN co-infection (HG type 0 present at 2,400 eggs/100 cc soil), poorly drained field areas identified in ArcGIS from tile spacing maps, (2) seed treatment comparison — ILeVO (fluopyram 0.15 mg a.i./seed) versus Saltro (pydiflumetofen) for SDS suppression with 6–8 bu/acre protection value at $14–18/unit treatment cost, (3) variety selection: JSB4880XF (SDS score 3.1, SCN resistance PI 88788) versus DS4710N (SDS score 2.3, Peking source SCN resistance) from 2022–2023 Iowa State ISA trial data, (4) planting date delay to May 12–18 targeting 55°F soil temp to reduce early-season infection period — yield cost of 0.4 bu/acre per day delay after May 10, (5) in-season scouting protocol: assess foliar SDS symptoms at R1 on 10 transects per field, document interveinal chlorosis vs. stem canker of SCN-caused root rot for accurate diagnosis. Produce a field-by-field risk rating and input investment decision tree.","tags":["pest-disease","soybean","SDS","IPM","seed-treatment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-22","title":"Corn Rootworm Refuge and Resistance Management Strategy","prompt":"You are an entomologist and certified crop adviser working with a continuous-corn grower in Bureau County, Illinois (1,100 acres, Muscatine-Sable complex, CEC 20 meq/100g) who has confirmed western corn rootworm (Diabrotica virgifera virgifera) variant resistance to Cry3Bb1 Bt traits on 4 of 11 fields based on 2023 larval damage ratings exceeding 1.0 on the Iowa 1–6 node injury scale. Design a resistance management and rootworm control program: (1) trait rotation — replace SmartStax (Cry3Bb1 + Cry34/35Ab1) with Optimum AcreMax TRIsect (Cry3Bb1 + Cry34/35Ab1 + Cry1A.105) plus rotate to Viptera corn on the 4 confirmed resistant fields, (2) soil insecticide evaluation on resistant fields: Force 3G (tefluthrin) at 6.4 oz/1,000 ft row versus Counter 20G (terbufos) with 5 bug days protection window — place in-furrow in a split application, (3) adult rootworm monitoring: yellow sticky traps (Pherocon AM yellow trap) at 1 trap per 10 acres beginning July 10, economic threshold 2.0 beetles per trap per day to trigger soil sampling for next-year risk assessment, (4) 4-year rotation plan for resistant fields: year 1 soybeans (breaks cycle completely), year 2 non-Bt corn + soil insecticide, year 3 trait pyramid with mCry3A, year 4 return to SmartStax, (5) economic impact calculation: rootworm control cost on 440 resistant acres at $28/acre (trait premium + soil insecticide) versus $61/acre uncontrolled yield loss at 35 bu/acre damage × $4.75/bu. Deliver a field-by-field action plan and 4-year rotation map.","tags":["pest-disease","rootworm","IPM","Bt-resistance","corn-entomology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-23","title":"Wheat Aphid Scouting and Biological Control Program","prompt":"You are a small grains entomologist advising a 1,400-acre winter wheat operation in Barton County, Kansas (Pratt loamy fine sand, dryland, avg yield 45 bu/acre) where greenbugs (Schizaphis graminum) and bird cherry-oat aphids (Rhopalosiphum padi) have caused 12% yield loss in 2 of the last 5 seasons. [Paste your crop scouting report] Build a season-long aphid IPM program: (1) fall scouting protocol: begin monitoring at Feekes 2 (3-leaf stage) using 100-tiller sample per 50-acre block, record aphid species, per-tiller counts, and parasitism rate (mummified aphids as %), economic threshold: 300 greenbugs per tiller at tillering without 15% parasitism, (2) beneficial insect habitat enhancement: plant 10-foot field border of sorghum-sudangrass and brown mustard as banker plant system to sustain Lysiphlebus testaceipes parasitoid and Orius predatory bug populations year-round, (3) insecticide decision matrix: if threshold exceeded and parasitism below 20%, apply Transform WG (sulfoxaflor 0.75 oz/acre) with 21-day residue window versus Dimethoate 4E as lower-cost backup — note sulfoxaflor pollinator risk, apply only when wheat not flowering, (4) BYDV transmission management: aphids at Feekes 3–6 are critical BYDV vectors — if >25 aphids per tiller before Feekes 6 in a high-BYDV risk year (late planting + fall rain), treat at lower threshold, (5) economic analysis: pyrethroids cost $4.50/acre, Transform $12.80/acre — evaluate cost versus expected yield loss from BYDV at 8 bu/acre × $6.20/bu = $49.60/acre potential loss. Produce a scouting log template and weekly decision calendar.","tags":["pest-disease","wheat","aphids","biological-control","Kansas"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-24","title":"Grape Berry Moth Management in Northeastern Vineyards","prompt":"You are a viticulture IPM specialist advising a 28-acre Riesling and Gewürztraminer vineyard in Yates County, New York (Finger Lakes AVA, Honeoye silt loam, pH 6.4) where grape berry moth (Paralobesia viteana) caused 22% cluster infestation in 2022 despite two broad-spectrum insecticide applications. Design a degree-day (DD) based IPM program: (1) biofix establishment — set biofix at first consistent adult capture (>5 moths per night) in Trécé Pherocon GBM lure delta trap, typically late April, accumulate DD using base 50°F from biofix, (2) generation timing: 1st generation egg hatch at 180 DD (target pre-bloom Bt application — Dipel DF at 0.5 lb/acre), 2nd generation at 810 DD (mid-summer, mating disruption active), 3rd generation at 1,440 DD (critical pre-veraison timing for Isomate GBM Rosso mating disruption), (3) mating disruption deployment: Isomate GBM Rosso dispensers at 200 per acre in early May before biofix, requires minimum 5-acre block size for effectiveness, (4) targeted insecticide applications at critical 180-DD and 810-DD windows using Altacor (chlorantraniliprole 3.5 oz/acre) for residual efficacy versus Delegate (spinetoram) as rotation partner, (5) infestation threshold assessment: count 100 clusters at pre-bloom, veraison, and harvest; 5% infested clusters at pre-bloom triggers intervention. Compare mating disruption ROI ($280/acre) versus spray program ($190/acre) against 2022 crop loss at $4,200/acre gross value. Produce a degree-day calendar and spray log template.","tags":["pest-disease","viticulture","grape-berry-moth","degree-day","IPM"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-25","title":"Late Blight Management Protocol for Commercial Potato Production","prompt":"You are a plant pathologist and extension specialist advising a 450-acre processing potato operation in Aroostook County, Maine (Caribou stony loam, pH 5.9, OM 3.7%) growing Atlantic and Caribou Russet varieties for McCain Foods. Phytophthora infestans late blight is endemic in the region and caused 30% yield loss in the 2021 epidemic year. [Paste your crop scouting report] Build a complete late blight management program: (1) forecasting system integration — subscribe to BlightPro (Cornell NEWA system) for field-level infection value accumulation; initiate fungicide program when cumulative infection values reach 18 or at first regional report, typically late June, (2) fungicide program — block 1 (pre-emergence to row closure): mancozeb (Dithane M-45 at 1.5 lb/acre) on 7-day schedule as protective base; block 2 (row closure to vine kill): alternate Orondis Opti (oxathiapiprolin + chlorothalonil), Revus Top (mandipropamid + difenoconazole), and Presidio (fluopicolide) on 5–7-day intervals based on infection values, (3) resistance management — mandate at least 3 FRAC group rotations per season to prevent metalaxyl resistance (established in Maine populations), (4) scouting protocol: walk 100-plant transects weekly from late June, record lesion presence, sporulation on underside, and distance from field edge to track spread direction, (5) vine kill timing: apply Diquat at 80–90% vine kill when tuber skin set is complete (tubers resist slipping at harvest), followed by final fungicide 48 hours later to prevent tuber infection via soil water. Calculate fungicide program cost per acre and cost per cwt for 350 cwt/acre yield.","tags":["pest-disease","potato","late-blight","fungicide","Maine"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-26","title":"Herbicide Resistance Management in Waterhemp-Infested Fields","prompt":"You are a weed scientist advising a 1,800-acre corn-soybean operation in McLean County, Illinois (Drummer silty clay loam) where common waterhemp (Amaranthus tuberculatus) has confirmed resistance to HPPD inhibitors (Group 27), ALS inhibitors (Group 2), PPO inhibitors (Group 14), and glyphosate (Group 9) — a 4-way resistant biotype confirmed by University of Illinois weed science lab in 2022. Design an integrated weed management plan: (1) residual herbicide foundation in corn: Acuron (bicyclopyrone + mesotrione + S-metolachlor + atrazine) at 2.5 qt/acre PRE within 3 days of planting, activated by 0.5 inch rain within 7 days to provide 5–6 week residual window before first POST application, (2) POST timing: Liberty (glufosinate) 29 oz/acre at waterhemp 2–4 inches (max 6 inches for efficacy) in LibertyLink GT27 corn, apply at 82°F max temperature with 1% AMS to prevent volatilization, (3) soybean program: Prefix (S-metolachlor + fomesafen) PRE at 2 qt/acre + XtendiMax 22 oz/acre POST at V1–V3 soybean in Xtend system — requires a 110-foot in-field buffer in temperatures exceeding 85°F, (4) non-chemical tactics: narrow row soybeans (15-inch) for 20% canopy closure advantage at 35 days, cover crops (cereal rye) to reduce waterhemp emergence by 40% through allelopathy and physical barrier, (5) seed bank depletion: zero seed production goal — hand-rogue any escapes above economic threshold of 1 plant per 10 ft², expected 30% seedbank decline per year. Produce an annual resistance management stewardship plan.","tags":["pest-disease","herbicide-resistance","waterhemp","weed-management","Illinois"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-27","title":"White Mold Sclerotinia Management in Edamame and Processing Soybeans","prompt":"You are a plant pathologist advising a 250-acre specialty edamame operation in Genesee County, New York (Honeoye silt loam, pH 6.7, OM 3.3%) under contract with a Japanese edamame processor at $0.38/lb pod weight. Sclerotinia stem rot caused 28% stand loss in 2022 on three high-humidity fields. Design a management program: (1) field risk assessment — soil sclerotia count by dilution plate method (SRSF agar medium) before planting; fields >10 sclerotia per gram soil are high risk, consider 2-year non-host rotation (corn or grain sorghum), (2) variety resistance screening: evaluate edamame varieties Sunrich Orange (moderate resistance), Beer Friend (susceptible), and Sayamusume (resistant per Cornell trial 2021–2023) for field placement based on risk mapping, (3) biological control: Contans WG (Coniothyrium minitans) at 1 kg/acre applied 3–4 months before planting to parasitize soil sclerotia — requires soil incorporation, (4) fungicide application timing at R1 (first flower) using Endura (boscalid 8 oz/acre) applied at 80% canopy closure with 14-day minimum to R3 retreatment window if wet conditions persist, (5) harvest moisture management: schedule harvest at 75–80% pod green stage (R6), avoid harvesting during morning dew, target pod moisture below 90% for wash-line pathogen control. Compare fungicide investment ($38/acre) ROI against 15% yield protection at $0.38/lb and 3,200 lb/acre expected yield.","tags":["pest-disease","white-mold","Sclerotinia","edamame","biological-control"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-28","title":"Nematode Management Strategy for High-Value Vegetable Production","prompt":"You are a nematologist and extension vegetable specialist advising a 40-acre certified organic tomato and pepper operation in Imperial County, California (Glenbar silty clay loam, pH 7.8, OM 0.8%) where Meloidogyne incognita (southern root-knot nematode) has damaged 35% of tomato plants for 3 consecutive seasons based on soil samples showing 850 eggs and second-stage juveniles per 100 cc soil — well above the economic threshold of 200 J2/100 cc. [Paste your crop scouting report] Design an organic nematode management program: (1) resistant variety deployment: Defiant PhR (Mi-1.2 gene) and Amelia (resistance confirmed 90% egg mass reduction in UC trials) for tomato, Keystone Resistant Giant pepper (N-gene resistance), (2) biofumigation with Indian mustard (Brassica juncea cv. Pacific Gold, 10 lb/acre) — mow at 25% bloom, incorporate to 8 inches within 30 minutes, apply 1.25 inches irrigation within 6 hours to activate glucosinolate hydrolysis to allyl isothiocyanate, (3) Burkholderia rinojensis (Majestene) applied in-furrow at 0.5 gal/acre at transplanting — 40–60% population suppression documented in Monterey County trials, (4) crop rotation: corn (non-host) or small grains for two seasons on highest-density fields before returning to Solanaceae, (5) post-harvest soil sampling at 0–12 inch and 12–24 inch depths to track population trends and assess management efficacy annually. Produce a 3-year rotation map and nematode population monitoring protocol.","tags":["pest-disease","nematode","organic","California","Meloidogyne"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-29","title":"Alfalfa Weevil Monitoring and Economic Threshold Scouting","prompt":"You are a forage entomologist advising a 600-acre alfalfa operation in Box Elder County, Utah (Fielding silty clay loam, pH 7.9, irrigated) that suffered first-cutting yield loss of 0.8 ton/acre in 2022 from alfalfa weevil (Hypera postica) defoliation on untreated fields. Build a degree-day based scouting and management protocol: (1) pest calendar: accumulate heat units base 48°F from January 1; adult oviposition begins at 200 DD, larvae hatch at 300 DD, peak larval damage at 450–600 DD — correlates to late April in northern Utah, (2) scouting protocol: collect 30 stems per field at 450 DD by bending stems into a bucket and counting larvae; economic threshold of 1–2 larvae per stem when alfalfa is 10–15 inches tall, (3) tip damage rating: classify fields as 25%, 50%, or 75% tip damage using 10-point visual scale; fields at 40%+ damage combined with 1.5 larvae per stem require immediate intervention, (4) management options: early harvest at 10-inch height removes 80% of larval population without insecticides — economic for first cutting only; chlorpyrifos (Lorsban 4E, 1 pt/acre) if >3 larvae per stem before cutting is feasible; Steward EC (indoxacarb) as pollinator-safer alternative at pre-bloom timing, (5) stem regrowth assessment at 7 and 14 days post-harvest: if crown buds show 50% browning, assess for crown weevil damage which requires Dimethoate application. Produce a scouting log template and economic threshold decision chart.","tags":["pest-disease","alfalfa","weevil","degree-day","IPM"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-30","title":"Spotted Wing Drosophila Management in Blueberry and Raspberry","prompt":"You are an entomologist at Michigan State University Extension advising a 35-acre u-pick berry operation in Leelanau County, Michigan (Rubicon sandy loam, pH 5.0 for blueberries) growing Bluecrop and Duke blueberries plus fall Heritage and Caroline red raspberries, where spotted wing drosophila (Drosophila suzukii) caused 30% culled fruit loss in 2021. Design an integrated SWD management program: (1) monitoring network: deploy Scentry SWD traps (ACV + wine lure) at 1 per 4 acres from green fruit stage through harvest; trap counts above 1 adult per trap per week at first fruit color indicates action threshold, (2) spray program timing: first spray at 5% blueberry fruit color change using Exirel (cyantraniliprole 13.5 oz/acre) on 7-day interval; rotate to Delegate WG (spinetoram 4.5 oz/acre) and Entrust SC (spinosad — OMRI-listed 6 oz/acre for organic) on 14-day rotation to manage resistance, (3) cultural controls: harvest every 3 days at peak ripeness to remove infestation sites, maintain 6-foot mowing strips to reduce humidity habitat, remove overripe and dropped fruit within 24 hours, (4) exclusion netting feasibility: 0.98mm white netting installed at first red stage — cost $8,200 for 5-acre blueberry block amortized 10 years = $820/year versus $2,400/year in losses; calculate block-by-block ROI, (5) post-harvest sanitation: destroy remaining fruit with disc incorporation within 7 days of final harvest to prevent overwintering population buildup. Produce a weekly spray and monitoring calendar for June through October.","tags":["pest-disease","SWD","blueberry","raspberry","Michigan"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-31","title":"Center Pivot Irrigation Scheduling for Corn in the Nebraska Sandhills","prompt":"You are an irrigation specialist with the University of Nebraska Extension advising a 640-acre center pivot corn operation in Cherry County, Nebraska (Valentine sandy loam, AWHC 1.1 in/ft, rooting depth 4 ft, maximum plant-available water 4.4 inches) with an 8-inch diameter well producing 800 GPM on a Valley 8000 series pivot with a 1,320-foot span. Design a season-long irrigation scheduling program: (1) evapotranspiration-based scheduling: subscribe to NebraskaET using nearby Ainsworth ET station (12 miles southeast) for daily ETo; apply crop coefficient (Kc) — vegetative Kc 0.3, peak Kc at tasseling 1.15, maturity Kc 0.6 — to calculate daily crop water use ETc, (2) soil water monitoring: install Sentek EnviroScan capacitance probes at 4 depths (12, 20, 28, 36 inches) in two representative zones (high and low yield ECa) calibrated to volumetric water content; trigger irrigation at 50% soil water depletion in top 24 inches, (3) pivot scheduling: 0.75 inch per pass at 100% timer setting = 72-hour revolution; calculate application efficiency at 88% distribution uniformity (DU) measured by catch-can test in 2023, (4) critical period priority: from VT to R3 (silking through blister), never allow soil water depletion to exceed 60% — add extra pass if 7-day ETc forecast exceeds 1.5 inches, (5) seasonal water budget: target 12 inches applied irrigation on 110-day corn; current well capacity = 800 GPM × 5,760 min/day ÷ 453 gal/acre-inch = 10.2 inch/season capacity — flag constraint and model yield reduction at 10 vs. 12 inches using DSSAT CropSim. Deliver a weekly pivot scheduling spreadsheet template.","tags":["irrigation","center-pivot","Nebraska","corn","ETc-scheduling"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-32","title":"Subsurface Drip Irrigation Design for Vegetable Production","prompt":"You are an irrigation engineer advising a 25-acre premium vegetable operation in Yolo County, California (Yolo clay loam, pH 7.2, bulk density 1.38 g/cm³) growing processing tomatoes, processing sweet corn, and fresh-market bell peppers in 3-year rotation under a UC Davis farm advisory. The existing overhead impact sprinkler system uses 38 acre-inches per season. Design a subsurface drip irrigation (SDI) conversion: (1) system layout: 60-inch raised beds with twin-row planting pattern, SDI tape at 18-inch depth below surface between rows at 60-inch spacing, Netafim Streamline 512 tape (0.22 GPH emitters, 12-inch spacing) with 8 mil wall thickness for 10-year lifespan, (2) hydraulic design: 8 zones × 3.2 acres per zone, 40 PSI operating pressure at zone header, flow rate 0.55 GPM/100 ft, design main line and submains for <5% head loss using Hazen-Williams equation with C=150 for PVC Schedule 40, (3) filtration system: 200-mesh disc filter (Arkal Spin Klin) + injection port for chlorination (3 ppm free chlorine at end of tape monthly) and acid flush (pH 5.5 for 30 minutes quarterly) to prevent iron and calcium carbonate emitter clogging, (4) fertigation program: daily fertigations at 0.35 EC set point for tomato, applying UAN-32 + potassium thiosulfate + chelated micronutrients through Dosatron 1:100 injector, (5) water savings verification: SDI application efficiency 92% versus sprinkler 72% → target 28 acre-inches applied versus 38 previously, saving $1,240/year at $55/acre-foot water cost. Deliver a system layout drawing description and annual maintenance schedule.","tags":["irrigation","drip-irrigation","California","fertigation","system-design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-33","title":"Tile Drainage Design and Water Table Management for Corn-Soybean Rotation","prompt":"You are a drainage engineer and agronomist advising a 480-acre corn-soybean operation in Defiance County, Ohio (Hoytville clay, drainage class very poorly drained, hydraulic conductivity 0.03 in/hr, average annual water surplus 18 inches) where delayed spring fieldwork from wet soils costs an estimated 14 bu/acre corn and 7 bu/acre soybean annually in late-planting yield drag. Design a comprehensive tile drainage upgrade: (1) drainage intensity target: remove 3/8-inch per day at 24-inch water table depth using Hooghoudt's equation with Ksat 0.03 in/hr → tile spacing of 35 feet for systematic pattern, (2) tile design: 4-inch perforated corrugated HDPE laterals at 35-foot spacing, 6-inch collector mains at 10-foot depth, outlets to two constructed drainage control structures (Agri Drain water control structures) for 2-way drainage water management (DWM), (3) drainage water management protocol: raise control structure boards to 18-inch depth from October 1 through April 15 to conserve water and reduce nitrate-N loss by 30%; lower to 4-foot depth from April 15 for spring drainage, (4) nitrate-N reduction credit: Ohio EPA H2Ohio DWM practice requires 30% nitrate reduction documentation — install a Hydro-Quip flow meter and SUNA nitrate sensor in outlet control structure for continuous monitoring, (5) economic analysis: tile installation cost $900/acre for 35-foot pattern; projected yield improvement 12 bu/acre corn ($57/acre) + 5 bu/acre soybeans ($55/acre) = $112/acre annual benefit; payback period 8 years. Produce a tile layout drawing description and DWM management calendar.","tags":["irrigation","tile-drainage","water-management","Ohio","DWM"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-34","title":"Micro-Irrigation Scheduling for Pecan Orchards in New Mexico","prompt":"You are an irrigation and tree crop specialist with NMSU Extension advising a 180-acre mature Wichita and Western pecan orchard in Doña Ana County, New Mexico (Glendale silty clay loam, pH 7.8, SAR 4.2, EC 1.8 dS/m, limiting sodium adsorption) under Rio Grande water rights of 3.2 acre-feet per acre per year. Average annual ET for mature pecans in Las Cruces is 74 inches. Design a micro-irrigation system and seasonal water management plan: (1) emitter placement: four 2-GPH pressure-compensating drippers per tree (Netafim PCJ-2) on 4 lateral lines per row at 3-foot radius from trunk, adjusted to 6-foot radius at full canopy coverage by year 8, (2) ETc calculation: reference ET from NMSU ET Toolbox (Las Cruces station) × Kc 1.1 during shell hardening (August) = peak daily demand 0.32 inches/tree/day, (3) salinity management: sodium toxicity threshold for pecan EC 4.0 dS/m — apply 15% leaching fraction monthly during May–September to maintain root zone EC below 3.0 dS/m; gypsum soil amendment at 1 ton/acre annually to displace sodium, (4) zinc nutrition: pecan requires 50+ ppm foliar Zn — apply chelated zinc sulfate at 2 lb/100 gal via airblast sprayer at bud break, leaf expansion, and nutlet set; monitor tissue zinc at 100+ mg/kg in July composite, (5) water rights compliance: log weekly water meter readings on NMSU WaterTRAX system and file OSE water use reports quarterly. Produce a monthly irrigation schedule from April through October with gallons per tree per day.","tags":["irrigation","pecan","New-Mexico","micro-irrigation","salinity"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-35","title":"Flood Irrigation Modernization for Alfalfa in the High Desert","prompt":"You are an irrigation efficiency specialist with the Natural Resources Conservation Service advising a 320-acre flood-irrigated alfalfa operation in Elko County, Nevada (Pronto silt loam, pH 7.6, average slope 0.3%, furrow irrigation from the Humboldt River water rights at 4.5 acre-feet/acre/year) that is losing an estimated 1.5 acre-feet/acre to deep percolation and tailwater losses. NRCS EQIP Practice 449 (Irrigation Water Management) and Practice 430 (Irrigation Pipeline) are available at 50% cost-share. Design a modernization plan: (1) tailwater recovery system: concrete settling basin 60×40×4 feet at field low end, 15 HP Gorman-Rupp sump pump to return 35% of applied water back to head ditch, estimated 1.2 acre-feet saved per acre per season, (2) laser leveling: survey field with Topcon 3D laser system and develop cut-fill map targeting 0.05% uniform slope — estimated soil movement 280 cy/acre at $4.20/cy, (3) surge valves: AquaBoss polyacetal surge valve on 12 main sockets switching on 8-minute cycle to advance water front across field 40% faster, reducing deep percolation in lead end of field by 30%, (4) pipeline conversion: replace 2,400 feet of open earthen ditch with 12-inch HDPE DR-21 buried pipeline, eliminating 15% conveyance loss and seepage, (5) cost-share calculation: total project cost $148,000 for 320 acres = $462/acre; NRCS 50% share = $231/acre; ROI from water savings 1.5 ac-ft × $65/ac-ft water cost = $97/acre/year; payback 2.4 years on farmer's cost. Produce a NRCS 319 application summary and project prioritization map.","tags":["irrigation","flood-irrigation","Nevada","NRCS-EQIP","water-conservation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-36","title":"Holstein Dairy Transition Cow Management Protocol","prompt":"You are a veterinarian and dairy nutritionist advising a 650-cow Holstein dairy in Tulare County, California (CDFA Grade A facility) where fresh cow disorders — milk fever (hypocalcemia) at 8.2%, displaced abomasum (DA) at 4.1%, and ketosis at 12.8% — are above industry benchmarks of 5%, 2.5%, and 8% respectively. These fresh cow problems cost approximately $420 per case in treatment, milk loss, and culling. Design a comprehensive transition cow program: (1) far-off dry cow nutrition (>21 days before calving): NDS Professional diet formulation targeting 14 Mcal NEL/day, 1,500 g RDP, DCAD of +100 to +150 mEq/kg DM using wheat straw dilution strategy, (2) close-up dry cow nutrition (0–21 days): dietary cation-anion difference (DCAD) of −100 to −150 mEq/kg DM using SoyChlor (anionic supplement) at 0.35 lb/head/day, targeting urine pH 6.0–6.5 monitored twice weekly, (3) periparturient calcium supplementation: oral calcium bolus (Bovikalc Blue, 86g calcium per dose) at calving plus 12 hours post-calving for all cows, with IV calcium gluconate 23% for clinical milk fever, (4) ketosis prevention: Propylene glycol 300 mL once daily from day 0 to day 14 post-fresh, supported by blood BHBA testing at days 3, 7, and 14 — treatment threshold >1.2 mmol/L, (5) fresh cow monitoring: train herdsperson to use Cowsense electronic health log — record rectal temperature, rumen fill score, manure consistency, and milk yield daily for 14 days; set alert triggers for ≥2 deviations from baseline. Produce a 21-day fresh cow monitoring scorecard and intervention decision tree.","tags":["livestock","dairy","transition-cow","nutrition","California"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-37","title":"Stocker Cattle Backgrounding Program for Southern Plains Wheat Pasture","prompt":"You are a livestock nutritionist and range specialist advising a 1,200-acre winter wheat and stocker cattle operation in Washita County, Oklahoma (Grant silt loam, pH 6.9, dryland wheat average 40 bu/acre). The operator stocked 480 crossbred stocker calves (avg 425 lb) on wheat pasture November 1 targeting 2.5 lb/day ADG for a 90-day winter grazing period before selling at 650 lb in late January. Design a complete backgrounding program: (1) receiving cattle protocol: 14-day receiving period before turnout on pasture — process with VISTA 5 + SOMNUS (respiratory and BVD 5-way vaccination), Dectomax injectable pour-on (1 ml/22 lb for internal/external parasites), and Ralgro implant (36 mg zeranol) for 8–12% ADG improvement, (2) wheat pasture stocking rate: 0.5 acre per 100 lb stocker per day (Oklahoma State Panhandle Research Station recommendation) = 480 calves × 4.25 AUM = 2,040 AUM; available pasture 900 acres × 2.3 AUM/acre = 2,070 AUM — adequate at this stocking rate, (3) bloat management: observe pasture for lush growth and dew conditions; administer poloxalene (Bloatguard) loose mineral at 2 oz/head/day when wheat is at joint elongation and lush green, (4) fescue endophyte-free forage supplement: where fescue has infiltrated wheat pastures, add 3 lb/head/day of dried distillers grains (27% CP, 90% TDN) to sustain ADG during cold stress below 32°F, (5) economic closeout: projected sale weight 650 lb × $1.85/lb = $1,202.50/head revenue; cost of gain calculation: purchase cost + vet + supplement + interest ÷ lbs gained = target <$0.98/lb. Produce a daily monitoring scorecard and economic projection spreadsheet.","tags":["livestock","stocker-cattle","wheat-pasture","Oklahoma","backgrounding"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-38","title":"Pasture-Based Hog Finishing System Design","prompt":"You are a swine specialist and sustainable agriculture consultant advising a 250-acre diversified farm in Lincoln County, Missouri (Menfro silt loam, pH 6.8, OM 2.4%) developing a pasture-based hog finishing enterprise targeting the premium natural pork market at $3.80/lb hanging weight through a 200-mile food-system distributor. Design the production system for 250 market hogs per year: (1) breed selection: Berkshire × Duroc cross for superior intramuscular fat (IMF 3.5–4.5%) and docile temperament; source feeder pigs from PIC 337 × Camborough cross as commercial backup at 50 lb entry weight, (2) paddock rotation system: 8 paddocks × 1.5 acres each = 12 total acres for 50-head groups; 3-week on/21-day rest rotation to prevent parasitism buildup and pasture degradation, rotational electric fencing with 3-strand Gallagher system at $0.18/running foot, (3) nutrition: base diet of corn-soybean meal (80% corn, 18% SBM, 2% vitamin-mineral premix) at 5–6% of bodyweight per day with 15% forage substitution from pasture reducing feed cost $12/head; target 2.5 FCR on 175-day finishing period, (4) waste management: hogs on pasture produce 4.5 lb manure per 100 lb BW per day — paddock rotation provides 21-day manure breakdown before re-entry; record field application rates and soil test annually to meet Missouri CAFO General Permit for operations <300 animal units, (5) market calculation: 250 hogs × 260 lb HCW × $3.80/lb = $247,000 gross; cost analysis at $1.95/lb cost of production = $123,500 variable costs; target $32,000 net income. Produce a paddock rotation schedule and annual production calendar.","tags":["livestock","swine","pasture-based","Missouri","premium-pork"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-39","title":"Layer Hen Flock Health and Biosecurity Program","prompt":"You are a poultry veterinarian and production specialist advising a 24,000-bird cage-free brown egg layer operation in Lancaster County, Pennsylvania (Kreider Farms supply chain, USDA Certified Humane certified) housing Hy-Line Brown hens in 4 aviary barns with 6,000 birds each. The operation has experienced two consecutive flocks with elevated early mortality (4.2% at 20 weeks versus 2.5% industry standard) and Marek's disease suspected in 15% of necropsy cases. Design a flock health and biosecurity program: (1) Marek's disease prevention: verify day-of-hatch vaccination status with hatchery (Rispens CVI 988 + HVT double vaccine standard); on-farm booster at 7 days post-arrival via wing-web stab with Marek's CVI 988 if purchase source uses HVT-only protocol, (2) biosecurity protocol: 72-hour downtime between flocks with litter composting in-barn at 140°F for 5 days minimum; Virkon S footbath at 1% concentration at every barn entrance changed every 2 days; 300-foot mandatory vehicle line for feed trucks, (3) respiratory disease monitoring: collect 30-bird blood samples at 16 weeks and 40 weeks for IB (infectious bronchitis) H120 and M41 serology; Newcastle HI titer >1:64 is protective threshold, revaccinate with Lasota strain if below 50% of flock, (4) nutrition for immune support: methionine at 420 mg/bird/day (0.45% diet) through pullet phase; vitamins A and E at 2× maintenance levels (8,000 IU/kg A and 20 IU/kg E) during stress periods (flock move, peak production), (5) mortality recording and USDA NPIP compliance: log daily mortality by pen, cause (necropsied versus not), and age; submit annual NPIP state flock test. Produce a 20-week pullet health calendar with vaccination schedule and biosecurity scorecard.","tags":["livestock","poultry","layers","biosecurity","Pennsylvania"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-40","title":"Cow-Calf Operation Reproductive Efficiency Improvement Plan","prompt":"You are a beef cattle reproduction specialist advising a 320-cow Angus-based cow-calf operation in Park County, Wyoming (native grass range, 7,200-foot elevation, 14-inch annual precipitation) with a 63-day spring calving season (March 1 – May 3) achieving a 78% pregnancy rate and 2.3 services per conception — significantly below the benchmark of 92% pregnancy rate. Develop a reproductive efficiency program: (1) herd body condition score (BCS) audit: assess all cows at weaning (October 1), pregnancy check (November 1), and at calving; target BCS 5.0–5.5 at calving, supplement thin cows (BCS <4.5) with 4 lb/day dry distillers grains (28% CP) over winter to recover condition before calving, (2) bull soundness evaluation (BSE): perform complete BSE on all bulls 60 days before turnout — motility >30%, morphology >70% normal, libido score 3+ — cull non-passing bulls; maintain 1 bull per 25 cows for natural service, (3) CIDR-based synchronization protocol for heifers: CIDR insert day 0, GnRH 100 mcg day 0, prostaglandin F2α day 7, GnRH day 9, fixed-time AI (FTAI) at 60 hours post-CIDR removal using Select Sires high-fertility semen, target 55–60% FTAI conception rate, (4) reproductive tract scoring (RTS) for yearling heifers: score at 12 months, cull heifers with RTS 1–2 (immature) and retain only RTS 3–5, targeting 85% heifers cycling before breeding, (5) economic analysis: improving pregnancy rate from 78% to 90% = 38 additional calves × $650 calf value = $24,700 additional gross per year versus $6,200 program cost. Produce a 12-month reproductive calendar and BSE evaluation form.","tags":["livestock","beef-cattle","reproduction","Wyoming","cow-calf"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-41","title":"No-Till Corn-Soybean Transition with Cover Crops and Carbon Metrics","prompt":"You are a regenerative agriculture specialist advising a 650-acre corn-soybean operation in Clinton County, Indiana (Brookston silty clay loam, pH 6.5, OM 3.2%, currently chisel-plowed annually) that wants to adopt no-till and cover crops to reduce input costs, improve soil health, and qualify for a Corteva Carbon program. [Describe your farm situation and current challenges] Design the 3-year transition: (1) Year 1: eliminate fall chisel plow; plant cereal rye (cereal rye 60 lb/acre + crimson clover 12 lb/acre) aerially at corn at R4; terminate with Roundup PowerMAX 44 oz/acre at rye Feekes 4.5, plant Green Gold corn hybrid in 30-inch rows with high-speed planter (Kinze 4900) at 32,500 seeds/acre with 10-15-0-14S starter at 4 gal/acre in-furrow, (2) Year 2: add Hairy vetch to cover crop mix for enhanced nitrogen fixation (50–80 lb N/acre); begin variable-rate fertilizer based on zone soil sampling; target OM increase of 0.1% verified by annual LECO analysis, (3) Year 3: convert 30-inch to 20-inch row spacing on soybeans to maximize canopy closure for weed suppression; establish field border habitat strips (pollinator mix) at 30-foot width on 3 field edges, (4) carbon program documentation: log all field operations in Climate FieldView Pro with precise geolocation; submit tillage intensity and cover crop adoption records to Corteva Granular Carbon platform; expected 0.35 Mg C/ha/year sequestration rate = 225 Mg CO₂e over 650 acres at $28/tonne = $6,300/year, (5) cost reduction tracking: target $22/acre reduction in fuel and tillage passes, $14/acre from reduced herbicide in year 3 from weed suppression. Produce a 3-year transition budget and carbon metric tracking spreadsheet.","tags":["regenerative","no-till","cover-crops","carbon-program","Indiana"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-42","title":"Agroforestry Silvopasture System Design for Beef and Timber","prompt":"You are an agroforestry specialist with the USDA National Agroforestry Center advising a 480-acre cow-calf operation in Franklin County, Virginia (Cecil sandy clay loam, pH 5.8, OM 2.1%, 12% average slope, erodible upland fields currently producing 2.1 tons/acre mixed fescue-orchardgrass hay) that wants to diversify income by integrating timber and livestock. Design a 20-year silvopasture system: (1) tree species selection: Eastern white pine (Pinus strobus) at $0.42/board foot final harvest value in Year 18 on well-drained ridges; black walnut (Juglans nigra) on mesic mid-slope positions at $12–18/board foot veneer quality in Year 25; chestnut (Castanea mollissima Chinese × C. dentata hybrid) on lower slopes for nut income starting Year 8 at $4.00/lb kernel value, (2) tree spacing and layout: 30×60-foot grid (24 trees/acre) for white pine and walnut on 80 acres; chestnut at 20×40-foot grid (54 trees/acre) on 40 acres; maintain 60% open canopy for warm-season grass understory pasture, (3) cattle integration: begin grazing understory after trees reach 8 feet with 4-foot tree guards; rotational grazing system with 6-week rest periods; stocking rate reduced 30% versus open pasture during first 5 years, (4) USDA RCPP payments: silvopasture qualifies for NRCS Practice 381 at $275/acre in Virginia; also eligible for EQIP Forestry (Practice 612) tree planting cost share at 50% of $1,200/acre establishment, (5) 20-year income projection: cattle income $280/acre/year ongoing; tree harvest Year 18–25 projected $8,400/acre NPV at 5% discount rate; total system NPV $4,200/acre versus $1,600/acre conventional pasture. Produce a planting map layout and 20-year cash flow projection.","tags":["regenerative","agroforestry","silvopasture","Virginia","timber"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-43","title":"Biologically Based Fertility Program for Organic Grain Production","prompt":"You are a certified organic crop consultant advising a 420-acre certified organic corn-soybean-small grain rotation in Dodge County, Wisconsin (Hochheim silt loam, pH 6.8, OM 4.1%, POXC 740 mg/kg, Haney score 16.4) that relies entirely on on-farm manure, cover crops, and OMRI-approved inputs. The farm targets 130 bu/acre organic corn at $9.20/bu, 40 bu/acre organic soybeans at $22/bu, and 70 bu/acre organic oats at $6.00/bu. Design the biological fertility program: (1) cover crop nitrogen credit system: hairy vetch + cereal rye mix killed at vetch 60% bloom contributes 80–110 lb N/acre; red clover (8 lb/acre aerial-seeded into small grain) returns 90 lb N/acre in Year 2 corn; calculate nitrogen balance replacing the eliminated 120 lb N/acre synthetic fertilizer program, (2) on-farm compost management: 500 tons composted beef manure annually (C:N 22:1) at 5 tons/acre applied to corn acres, providing 35 lb plant-available N/acre in Year 1; record PFRP (Process to Further Reduce Pathogens) temperature log (55°C for 3 consecutive days, 5 turns) required for organic certification, (3) biological inoculants: Bradyrhizobium japonicum (XA inoculant at 3 oz/cwt seed) for soybeans targeting 65% biological N fixation; Azospirillum brasilense (Envita at 3.5 fl oz/cwt) for corn BNF supplementation of 30–45 lb N/acre verified by ¹⁵N isotope dilution, (4) phosphorus and potassium management: rock phosphate (0-3-0, 32% P₂O₅) at 200 lb/acre on fields below Bray P1 15 ppm; sulfate of potash at 100 lb K₂O/acre on fields below 150 ppm K, (5) economic comparison: organic corn total production cost $485/acre versus $410/acre conventional; $9.20/bu × 130 bu = $1,196/acre gross; $711/acre net versus $480/acre conventional net. Produce a 3-year fertility budget and organic certification compliance checklist.","tags":["regenerative","organic","biological-fertility","Wisconsin","grain"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-44","title":"Holistic Planned Grazing System for Diverse Ranch Restoration","prompt":"You are a certified Holistic Management educator and rangeland specialist advising a 4,200-acre cow-calf and yearling stocker operation in Custer County, South Dakota (Pierre clay and Faulkton loam mix, 16-inch annual precipitation, 72% range condition poor to fair) where overgrazing and continuous season-long grazing over 40 years has resulted in blue grama and buffalo grass replacement by western snowberry, yucca, and annual bromes. Design a 5-year holistic planned grazing program: (1) land assessment and paddock design: identify 18 grazing cells using topographic maps and water source locations; each cell 230 acres average; target 60-day recovery period in growing season (May–September) = 18 cells × 4 days grazing each = 72-day rotation cycle, (2) stock density increase: current 150 cow-calf pairs at 28 acres/AU = 0.036 AU/acre; holistic target of 120 AU/acre impact day for 3 days per cell to stimulate plant root sloughing and biological decay of standing dead material, (3) annual plan development: use Holistic Management International planning chart; map water distribution, estimate forage demand in AUDs (animal unit days), and schedule quarterly observation windows to verify plant recovery, (4) monitoring protocol: Robel pole visual obstruction reading at 10 fixed photopoints per cell twice yearly; Parker Loop transect for basal cover and litter cover percentage at 20 points per cell annually, (5) 5-year restoration targets: increase perennial grass basal cover from 12% to 35%; reduce snowberry canopy from 28% to 8% through high-density impact grazing in dormant season; increase rangeland condition score from 38 to 65 by Year 5. Produce a paddock map design and annual AUD planning worksheet.","tags":["regenerative","holistic-grazing","rangeland","South-Dakota","restoration"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-45","title":"Carbon Farming Plan Using Compost Application and Rangeland Restoration","prompt":"You are an agronomist and carbon program specialist advising a 3,200-acre rangeland and dryland grain operation in Marin County, California (Denison clay loam, pH 6.1, SOC 1.82%, average annual precipitation 26 inches) enrolling in a Marin Carbon Project protocol. The operator wants to apply one-time compost application to 800 acres of degraded annual grassland to stimulate perennial grass regeneration and carbon sequestration. Design the program: (1) compost application design: apply mature (>150-day) dairy compost at 0.5 dry ton/acre broadcast applied in October before first rains; compost analysis target: OM 45%, C:N 18:1, pH 7.2, EC 4.5 dS/m, no heavy metals above CDFA standards, (2) soil sampling: collect 0–10 cm and 10–30 cm depth samples at 40 stratified random points before application and at Years 1, 3, and 5; analyze for SOC (dry combustion LECO), bulk density (cores), and 13C isotope ratio to partition old versus new carbon, (3) plant community monitoring: 50-meter Daubenmire transects at 30 fixed points — record perennial grass cover, annual grass cover, forb cover, and bare ground percentage; target shift from 8% perennial grass to 30% by Year 5, (4) carbon quantification: Marin Carbon Project protocol predicts 0.9–1.6 Mg CO₂e/ha/year over 10 years on treated grassland; conservative estimate 1.0 Mg × 800 acres × 0.405 ha/acre × $28/tonne = $9,072/year revenue after 20% buffer pool deduction, (5) regulatory reporting: submit annual monitoring data to Marin RCD and California Healthy Soils Program; claim Healthy Soils Program CDFA incentive payment of $15/dry ton compost cost-share. Produce a soil sampling map and carbon monitoring protocol.","tags":["regenerative","carbon-farming","rangeland","California","compost"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-46","title":"Farm Profitability Analysis for Corn-Soybean Operation Transitioning to Specialty Crops","prompt":"You are an agricultural economist advising a 480-acre corn-soybean farm in LaGrange County, Indiana (currently averaging 195 bu/acre corn at $4.85/bu and 54 bu/acre soybeans at $12.20/bu) with $285/acre average net income in 2022–2023. The operator wants to analyze converting 80 acres to identity-preserved popcorn and 60 acres to edible white beans under contract to a specialty grain elevator. [Describe your farm situation and current challenges] Build a complete enterprise budget comparison: (1) IP popcorn enterprise budget: seed $82/acre (Preferred Seed HIJK-47), contract price $11.50/cwt, target 5,200 lb/acre = $598/acre gross; additional costs: $38/acre for Biflex-Ultra herbicide program, $22/acre for grain cleaner rental and identity preservation segregation, $18/acre crop insurance premium for specialty crop endorsement; net $440/acre versus $210/acre conventional corn, (2) white bean enterprise budget: seed $68/acre (Berkshire variety), contract at $35/cwt, target yield 2,000 lb/acre = $700/acre gross; additional costs: fungicide (Endura 8 oz/acre $42), harvest adjustments (reel speed, ground speed) and extra bin space $15/acre; net $480/acre versus $265/acre soybeans, (3) risk analysis: specialty crop yield CV (coefficient of variation) 22% versus commodity corn 12%; crop insurance APH calculation for first-year specialty acre; contract cancellation risk — evaluate 3-year contract with penalty clause for delivery default, (4) marketing strategy: identify 2 backup buyers within 150 miles and forward contract 70% of production at signing, leave 30% for harvest-price opportunity, (5) cash flow projection: specialty crop revenue delays 60–90 days past harvest for quality testing and elevator settlement — model operating line of credit needs during October–December period. Produce a side-by-side enterprise budget worksheet and risk decision matrix.","tags":["farm-business","enterprise-budget","specialty-crops","Indiana","economics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-47","title":"Whole-Farm Financial Stress Test and Debt Restructuring Analysis","prompt":"You are an agricultural lender and farm financial analyst advising a 1,200-acre corn-soybean operation in Marshall County, Iowa that purchased 480 acres at $10,800/acre in 2021 financed at 4.85% over 20 years ($32,800/year principal + interest), holds $280,000 in operating line at 7.9% ($22,120 interest), and carries $140,000 in equipment debt at 5.9%. The 2023 operating loss was $42,000 after a 22% corn price decline. [Describe your farm situation and current challenges] Build a financial stress test and restructuring plan: (1) current ratio and working capital analysis: compile current assets (grain inventory $186,000, prepaid inputs $48,000, accounts receivable $22,000) versus current liabilities (operating note $280,000, current portion LTD $28,400) = current ratio 0.91 — below 1.0 danger threshold, working capital deficit $52,400, (2) debt service coverage ratio (DSCR): net farm income $62,000 + depreciation $58,000 + family living withdrawal $48,000 = $168,000 available; total debt service $82,900 = DSCR 2.03 — adequate but declining, (3) restructuring options: extend land loan from 20 to 30 years (reduces annual payment to $21,200, saves $11,600/year); FSA Direct Farm Ownership loan restructure at 4.5%; roll operating line balance into term note at 5.5% over 7 years to improve working capital, (4) revenue improvement levers: cash rent renegotiation on 420 rented acres — model 3 scenarios ($215, $225, $235/acre) versus current $248/acre average; custom farming 200 acres neighboring operation at $48/acre net; sell 280-acre parcel at $10,200/acre to retire land debt on remaining 200 acres, (5) 3-year recovery roadmap with annual benchmarks: target working capital >$80,000 by Year 2, DSCR >2.5 by Year 3, debt-to-asset ratio below 0.42 by Year 3. Produce a restructuring scenario comparison table and 5-year cash flow projection.","tags":["farm-business","farm-finance","debt-restructuring","Iowa","financial-analysis"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-48","title":"Direct-to-Consumer Beef Marketing Program for Grass-Fed Operation","prompt":"You are an agricultural marketing specialist advising a 220-acre grass-fed beef operation in Hardin County, Tennessee (Decatur silt loam, pH 6.5, OM 2.8%) raising 80 Angus-Hereford crossbred steers on native warm-season grasses, finishing at 24–26 months at 1,050 lb live weight and 650 lb HCW. The operation wants to shift from auction barn sales at $1.45/lb live weight to direct-consumer sales at $7.50/lb hanging weight through a USDA-inspected facility. Design the marketing program: (1) USDA-inspected processor selection: identify and vet local USDA-custom-exempt processors within 90 miles — evaluate kill fee ($250–$350/head), cut-and-wrap fee ($0.70–$0.90/lb HCW), and scheduling availability for 2 head per week steady flow, (2) product mix and pricing: quarter, half, and whole beef packages — whole beef 650 lb HCW × 70% cutting yield = 455 lb retail cuts at $7.50/lb HCW = $4,875/head; compare to auction $1,522/head — net premium $3,353/head × 80 head = $268,240 additional gross, (3) customer acquisition: launch Instagram and Facebook farm page with monthly butchering process videos; target 200-mile CSA-style beef subscription radius; partner with 3 Nashville farmers markets for deposit sales and pickup points; target 40 whole-beef, 60 half-beef orders to absorb annual production, (4) cold storage infrastructure: 8×20-foot walk-in cooler at $18,400 installed for aging inventory 10–14 days post-processing, retail chest freezers 4×22 cu ft for market day sales, food safety plan under Tennessee Dept of Agriculture cottage food or mobile unit inspection, (5) AMS Livestock reporting: register operation with USDA Agricultural Marketing Service for grass-fed claim; compile grazing records demonstrating 100% forage diet for USDA Process Verified certification ($1,800 audit). Produce a customer acquisition timeline and break-even analysis at varying order mix scenarios.","tags":["farm-business","direct-marketing","grass-fed-beef","Tennessee","direct-to-consumer"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-49","title":"Agritourism Enterprise Development for Diversified Family Farm","prompt":"You are an agritourism development specialist advising a 180-acre diversified vegetable and orchard operation in Door County, Wisconsin (Emmet silt loam, pH 6.7) growing 85 varieties of heritage apples and 40 acres of market vegetables that currently sells wholesale to 3 distributors at low margins. The farm wants to develop agritourism to capture value-added revenue on-farm. Design a comprehensive agritourism enterprise: (1) infrastructure assessment and investment: convert existing 2,400 sq ft equipment barn into event and retail barn at $68,000 renovation cost — commercial kitchen (NSF-approved, $22,000), retail space with walk-in cooler, and 150-person event capacity with ADA-compliant bathrooms and parking for 60 cars, (2) product and experience portfolio: u-pick apples September–October at $2.50/lb (industry average margin 3.2× wholesale), farm-to-table dinner events at $85/person 8× per season for 60 guests = $40,800, school educational tours at $12/student targeting 40 groups = $19,200, apple cider pressing weekends with 5-gallon unpasteurized cider sales at $28/jug under Wisconsin Farm Market Exemption, (3) regulatory compliance: Wisconsin DATCP agritourism liability exemption (Wis. Stat. 895.527) requires posted warning signs; retail food establishment license $350/year; liability insurance rider $1,800/year for u-pick, events, and farm tours, (4) digital marketing system: Google Business Profile optimization for 'Door County apple orchard'; Airbnb Experiences listing for cider pressing; Eventbrite platform for ticketed dinners; email list from 2,400 existing wholesale buyers as initial marketing base, (5) 5-year revenue projection: Year 1 agritourism gross $85,000; Year 3 $145,000; Year 5 $210,000 — compare to current wholesale margin of $62,000 net. Produce a launch timeline and investment payback analysis.","tags":["farm-business","agritourism","Wisconsin","direct-sales","value-added"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-50","title":"Crop Insurance Strategy for Diversified Midsize Grain Farm","prompt":"You are an agricultural risk management specialist advising a 900-acre corn-soybean-wheat operation in Shelby County, Ohio (Kokomo silty clay loam, tile-drained, APH: corn 198 bu/acre, soybean 56 bu/acre, wheat 72 bu/acre) navigating USDA Risk Management Agency crop insurance options for 2024. The farm carries $2.1M in commodity revenue exposure and lost $84,000 in 2022 from a prevented planting event covered at 70% APH. [Describe your farm situation and current challenges] Build a complete insurance strategy: (1) coverage level optimization analysis: compare 75% APH RP (Revenue Protection) versus 80% APH RP versus 85% SCO (Supplemental Coverage Option) add-on for corn and soybeans — tabulate premium cost, expected indemnity trigger price, and break-even coverage level at $4.75/bu corn futures, (2) prevented planting analysis: 2022 loss at $84,000 covered $61,200 at 70% PP; upgrade to 80% PP coverage for corn on wet tile-drained fields — additional premium cost $8,400 versus $18,000 additional coverage, (3) Whole-Farm Revenue Protection (WFRP): evaluate WFRP at 80% coverage as alternative to commodity RP — gross revenue guarantee $1,680,000; premium $24,600 — compare to commodity RP portfolio cost $38,200, (4) ARC-CO versus PLC election: model both 2024 Farm Bill program elections using USDA FSA ARC/PLC calculator — ARC-CO corn payments on 320 base acres if MYA price falls below Olympic average $4.85; PLC corn provides guaranteed payment if MYA below $3.70 reference price, (5) marketing strategy integration: align crop insurance coverage election with forward contract position — if 40% of crop is sold forward at $5.10/bu, downgrade corn RP to 75% to reduce premium cost since forward contracts provide first loss protection. Produce a 3-scenario insurance comparison table and net cost per bushel analysis.","tags":["farm-business","crop-insurance","risk-management","Ohio","RMA"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},
  {"id":"agr-51","title":"Industrial Hemp CBD Production Plan for Transitioning Tobacco Ground","prompt":"You are an agronomist and hemp production specialist advising a 280-acre diversified farm in Scott County, Kentucky (Maury silt loam, pH 6.6, OM 2.9%) that grew burley tobacco for 30 years and wants to convert 40 acres to hemp for CBD flower production under a Kentucky Department of Agriculture hemp license. Design a full production plan: (1) variety selection — use feminized seed from Cherry Wine, Wife, or Otto II varieties testing below 0.3% delta-9 THC at harvest; source from KDA-approved seed suppliers; feminized transplant cost $1.20–$1.80/plant at 1,800 plants/acre = $2,160–$3,240/acre establishment, (2) transplant production — start seedlings in 72-cell trays in existing tobacco float bed greenhouse; transplant at 6-inch height after last frost (May 10 average for Scott County) into plastic-mulched raised beds with drip tape at 18-inch spacing, (3) fertility and irrigation — apply 80 lb N/acre split between transplanting (30%) and bud initiation (70%) as calcium nitrate through drip; maintain soil moisture at 65–70% field capacity during flowering to maximize cannabinoid content, (4) compliance management — mandatory THC testing by KDA-approved lab at 15 days pre-harvest; fields testing 0.31–1.0% THC must be remediated or destroyed; maintain GPS field records and chain-of-custody documentation for all lots, (5) harvest and drying — cut at peak CBD:THC ratio (typically 8–10% CBD); hang-dry in converted tobacco barn at 60°F and 60% RH for 14–21 days to target 10–12% moisture; projected yield 800–1,200 lb dry flower/acre at $150–$250/lb wholesale = $120,000–$300,000/acre gross range. Produce a compliance calendar and enterprise budget with three price scenarios.","tags":["specialty-crops","hemp","Kentucky","CBD","crop-production"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-52","title":"Hop Yard Establishment and Trellising System for Craft Brewery Supply","prompt":"You are a hop production specialist advising a 120-acre diversified grain and livestock farm in Yakima County, Washington (Roza silt loam, pH 6.8, OM 1.6%, irrigated from Roza Irrigation District) adding 8 acres of hops under a 3-year purchase agreement with a Spokane craft brewery at $4.50/lb dried hops for Cascade and Centennial varieties. Design the production system: (1) trellis installation — 18-foot hop trellis system using 6-inch treated Douglas fir poles at 80-foot intervals; 12-gauge galvanized wire strung between poles; total materials and installation $12,500/acre; pole-and-wire system expected 20-year lifespan, (2) rhizome planting — source USDA-certified disease-free Cascade rhizomes (8 lb/acre) and Centennial rhizomes from Yakima Chief Hops nursery at $2.20/rhizome; plant in April at 3.5×7-foot in-row spacing (1,782 hills/acre) in 18-inch raised beds with 30-inch drip tape lateral spacing, (3) first-year training and irrigation — manually train 2–3 bines per string clockwise in late May; apply 22 inches of water through drip from April–September; nitrogen program: 60 lb N/acre as UN-32 split between March soil application and post-harvest fertigation, (4) disease management — downy mildew (Pseudoperonospora humuli) is primary threat; apply Aliette 80WDG at 5 lb/acre preventively at bud swell and every 10 days during wet periods; Phytophthora crown rot managed with Ridomil Gold at crown zone in spring, (5) harvest logistics — Cascade matures mid-August; whole-cone harvest requires mobile harvester rental at $1,800/day for 2 days; kiln-dry to 8–10% moisture in converted grain dryer at 140°F maximum; Year 3 projected yield 1,800 lb/acre × $4.50 = $8,100/acre gross. Produce a 5-year cash flow showing establishment investment recovery timeline.","tags":["specialty-crops","hops","Washington","craft-brewery","trellis"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-53","title":"Ginseng Production System for Woodland Forest Floor Cultivation","prompt":"You are a specialty crop consultant advising a 320-acre mixed hardwood and grain farm in Pendleton County, West Virginia (Dekalb channery loam, pH 5.4, OM 4.8%, north-facing slopes under second-growth oak-hickory canopy providing 75–80% shade) that wants to establish 2 acres of American ginseng (Panax quinquefolius) for the export dried root market. Design the woodland cultivation system: (1) site preparation — select north- and east-facing slopes with 70–80% natural canopy shade; remove invasive multiflora rose and Japanese barberry mechanically; thin canopy to achieve 75% shade using densiometer readings; build raised beds 4 feet wide × 6 inches high using native leaf litter and composted hardwood bark at pH 5.2–5.8, (2) seed stratification and planting — source stratified seed from Appalachian Botanical Company at $65–$75/lb; plant September–October at 1-inch depth, 6-inch spacing in rows 12 inches apart; seeding rate 20 lb stratified seed/acre; expect 40–60% germination in spring Year 1, (3) disease management — Phytophthora root rot and Alternaria blight are primary risks; apply copper hydroxide (Champ DP) at 3 lb/acre at emergence and every 14 days through June; maintain 60-second drainage in raised beds to prevent waterlogging; remove and bag diseased plants immediately, (4) harvest timing — roots require 5–7 years to reach marketable size (3/8-inch neck diameter, 1.5 oz fresh weight); hand-dig in September Year 5–7; wash and dry at 95°F for 3 weeks to 10–12% moisture; projected yield 300–500 lb dried root/acre/year at $400–$650/lb for wild-simulated grade, (5) CITES export compliance — American ginseng requires USFWS CITES Appendix II export permit; contact WV DNR for state harvest certificate; maintain harvester records for federal compliance. Produce a 7-year cash flow and investment return analysis at $500/lb average price.","tags":["specialty-crops","ginseng","Appalachian","woodland-farming","agroforestry"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-54","title":"Elderberry Orchard Establishment for Value-Added Product Market","prompt":"You are a small fruit agronomist advising a 95-acre diversified farm in Franklin County, Missouri (Menfro silt loam, pH 6.5, OM 2.4%) establishing 6 acres of elderberry (Sambucus nigra subsp. canadensis) for sale to a local elderberry syrup manufacturer at $1.20/lb fresh berry and direct retail at $3.50/lb for dried berries. Design the establishment and management system: (1) variety selection and layout — plant Bob Gordon (highest Missouri yield trial average at 12,000 lb/acre fresh) and Wyldewood varieties in alternating rows for cross-pollination; 5×10-foot spacing (870 plants/acre) on contour with grass-sod middles; plant bare-root whips in April from River Hills Harvest nursery at $3.80/plant = $3,306/acre establishment plant cost, (2) fertility and weed management — apply composted poultry litter at 2 tons/acre broadcast before planting; maintain 4-foot weed-free strip with landscape fabric first 2 years; switch to mow-and-blow management Year 3; annual N application 60 lb N/acre as 30-0-0 liquid urea split April/June, (3) pruning system — cane renewal pruning each March: remove all canes older than 3 years; maintain 4–6 first-year, 4–6 second-year, and 4–6 third-year canes per clump; hedgerow height capped at 8 feet for ease of harvest, (4) pest and disease management — elderberry is relatively low-input; monitor for elder shoot borer (Desmocerus palliatus) by scouting for wilted shoot tips in June; remove and destroy infested canes; Japanese beetle defoliation managed with Surround WP kaolin clay during peak flight (late June–July) for organic-compatible management, (5) harvest and marketing — harvest by hand or with modified grape harvester in August–September when 95% of berries in cluster are dark purple; Year 3 projected yield 4,000 lb/acre; Year 5 full production 10,000–12,000 lb/acre; enterprise budget at $1.20/lb: $14,400/acre gross Year 5 versus $6,200/acre total costs. Produce a 5-year establishment budget and revenue projection.","tags":["specialty-crops","elderberry","Missouri","value-added","small-fruit"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-55","title":"Lavender Farm Production and Agritourism Integration Plan","prompt":"You are a specialty crop and agritourism consultant advising a 75-acre farm in the Willamette Valley, Oregon (Jory silty clay loam, pH 6.3, OM 3.8%, well-drained upland slopes) that wants to establish 5 acres of lavender (Lavandula angustifolia) for essential oil distillation, dried bundles, and u-pick agritourism. Design the integrated enterprise: (1) variety selection and establishment — plant Hidcote (high oil content, 0.9–1.2% essential oil yield) and Vera (culinary and bundle market) from rooted cuttings at 3×5-foot spacing (2,904 plants/acre); plant in April on 8-inch raised beds with weed barrier; cuttings from Nichols Farm Nursery at $1.85/plant = $5,372/acre; avoid wet soils — lavender requires 12-inch tile drainage or natural slope drainage, (2) essential oil production — distill Hidcote at peak bloom (10% open flowers) using on-farm copper pot still (500-liter capacity, $14,000); fresh plant material yield 200 lb essential oil/acre at full production Year 3–4; wholesale to soap and cosmetics manufacturers at $120–$180/lb; direct retail at $280/lb, (3) dried bundle market — harvest Vera at bud stage before full open; hand-tie in 50-stem bundles; dry in shaded barn at 85°F for 10 days; retail at $12–$18/bundle at Portland Saturday Market and Etsy; target 800 bundles/acre = $11,200/acre retail gross, (4) u-pick agritourism — open farm Saturday–Sunday during 3-week peak bloom (late June); charge $18/person admission plus $8/lb u-pick; Willamette Valley lavender farms average 2,400 visitors/season generating $28,000–$45,000 in admission and on-farm retail sales; comply with Benton County agritourism liability posting under ORS 30.970, (5) social media marketing — Instagram and Pinterest are primary channels for lavender; post daily during bloom; partner with Portland wedding planners for fresh-cut flower bouquets at $65/arrangement. Produce a 4-year enterprise budget with agritourism and product revenue streams modeled separately.","tags":["specialty-crops","lavender","Oregon","agritourism","essential-oil"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-56","title":"Vertical Farm Lettuce Production System Design and Economics","prompt":"You are a controlled environment agriculture engineer advising an investor group planning a 10,000-square-foot vertical farm in a former warehouse in Columbus, Ohio, targeting foodservice and grocery supply of living butter lettuce, romaine, and arugula at $2.80–$3.40/lb wholesale. Design the complete production system and financial model: (1) growing system selection — nutrient film technique (NFT) on 6-tier vertical racks with 8-inch channel spacing; 40,000 plant sites per 10,000 sq ft; GrowFlux Flex8 LED fixtures at 200 µmol/m²/s PPFD, 18-hour photoperiod, targeting 35–40 DLI; electricity consumption 42 kWh/sq ft/year at $0.08/kWh = $33,600/year lighting cost, (2) production cycle — butter lettuce transplanted from 21-day seedling stage at 4-inch spacing; harvest at 35 days in system; 10 turns/year; 40,000 heads × 0.35 lb average = 14,000 lb/turn × 10 = 140,000 lb/year production capacity, (3) nutrient management — recirculating Hoagland solution modified for lettuce: EC 1.8–2.2 mS/cm, pH 5.8–6.2; drain-to-waste 10% daily to prevent salt accumulation; water use 1.2 gallons/lb produce versus 30 gallons/lb field production; RO water pretreatment to remove chloramine at $8,400 capital cost, (4) labor model — 3.5 FTE at $18/hour including seeding, transplanting, harvesting, packaging, and sanitation; automate seeding with Hamilton Beach seeder at $12,000 to reduce FTE by 0.5; total labor $126,000/year, (5) financial model — total capital $1.2M (racking $280,000, lighting $340,000, HVAC $180,000, fertigation $90,000, buildout $310,000); operating cost $38,500/month; break-even at 72% capacity utilization = 100,800 lb/year; at full capacity 140,000 lb × $3.10/lb = $434,000/year gross; Year 5 projected EBITDA $86,000. Produce a detailed capital budget, monthly operating pro forma, and sensitivity analysis on electricity price and lettuce price.","tags":["controlled-environment","vertical-farming","lettuce","Columbus","CEA-economics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-57","title":"Greenhouse Strawberry Production Using Dutch Tabletop System","prompt":"You are a protected agriculture specialist advising a 3-acre greenhouse operation in Monterey County, California (existing gutter-connected Venlo-style greenhouse, natural gas heating) converting from cut flowers to strawberry production using Dutch tabletop elevated trough system to supply a premium grocery chain at $4.20/lb year-round. Design the conversion: (1) tabletop system installation — elevated aluminum gutters at 36-inch working height; 2.8 plants/linear foot; 62,000 plants per acre of greenhouse floor; coir substrate (pH 5.8–6.2, EC 1.0 prewetted) in 1-gallon grow bags; system cost $85,000/acre installed including benching, drip lines, and substrate, (2) variety selection — day-neutral varieties Albion and Monterey for year-round production; Cabrillo for summer peak; source cold-stored runner plants from UC Davis Foundation Plant Services ($0.62/plant) for September plug planting and February replant on staggered 8-month production cycle, (3) fertigation program — drip at 4–6 times daily at 50 mL/event/plant; Haifa Multi-K GG potassium nitrate base nutrient solution; adjust calcium nitrate during fruit set (CaNO₃ at 200 ppm Ca²⁺); EC 1.8–2.2 mS/cm; drain-to-waste 20% monitored for nutrient runoff compliance under Monterey County NPDES permit, (4) integrated pest management — two-spotted spider mite (Tetranychus urticae) primary pest; preventive Phytoseiulus persimilis releases at 50 mites/plant every 2 weeks; Botrytis managed with 6-hour daily ventilation periods and Bacillus subtilis (Serenade) at 4 oz/100 gal weekly; bumblebee hives (Koppert Natupol) for pollination at 1 hive/2,500 sq ft, (5) production economics — 62,000 plants × 2.2 lb/plant/year = 136,400 lb/acre/year; at $4.20/lb = $572,880/acre gross; operating cost $380,000/acre; net $192,880/acre. Produce a month-by-month production schedule and harvest calendar.","tags":["controlled-environment","greenhouse","strawberry","California","tabletop-system"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-58","title":"Precision Drip Irrigation Design for High-Value Vegetable Production","prompt":"You are an irrigation engineer advising a 45-acre intensive vegetable farm in Yuma County, Arizona (Superstition loamy sand, FC 12%, PWP 4%, AWHC 0.96 in/ft, Colorado River water at EC 1.1 dS/m) growing iceberg lettuce, broccoli, and cantaloupe in a 3-crop rotation with 4 production cycles per year. Design a precision drip irrigation system: (1) system hydraulic design — mainline 4-inch polyethylene at 40 psi operating pressure; submains 2-inch at 30 psi; drip laterals 5/8-inch Netafim UniRam with pressure-compensating emitters at 12-inch spacing, 0.42 GPH; design for 0.10 in/hour application rate to match sandy loam infiltration rate; system uniformity target DU > 92%, (2) ET-based scheduling — use AZMET Arizona Meteorological Network daily ETo data for Yuma station; crop coefficients: lettuce Kc 0.35–1.0 (emergence to maturity); broccoli Kc 0.35–1.1; cantaloupe Kc 0.35–1.0; schedule irrigations to replace 100–110% of daily ETc; lettuce peak demand 0.22 in/day in March requiring 90-minute run time at 0.42 GPH emitter spacing, (3) fertigation integration — inject AquaFert calcium nitrate at 75 ppm N and potassium thiosulfate (0-0-25-17S) through Dosatron proportional injector at 1:200 ratio; maintain injector downstream of backflow preventer per Arizona DEQ requirements; flush laterals with clean water for final 10 minutes of each cycle, (4) salinity management — leaching requirement at EC 1.1 Colorado River water: LR = ECw/(5×ECe-ECw) = 0.15; apply 15% excess irrigation for salt leaching; monitor soil ECe monthly with EM38 electromagnetic induction survey, (5) water accounting — total seasonal water use: lettuce 18 acre-inches, broccoli 22 acre-inches, cantaloupe 32 acre-inches; 45 acres × 3 crops × average 24 acre-inches = 3,240 acre-feet/year; water cost at $42/acre-foot = $135,000/year; drip system reduces water use 35% versus furrow = $47,250 annual savings. Produce a system design diagram schematic and water budget worksheet.","tags":["irrigation","drip-irrigation","Arizona","water-management","vegetable-production"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-59","title":"Center Pivot Irrigation Scheduling and Variable Rate Application","prompt":"You are a precision irrigation specialist advising a 2,400-acre corn and alfalfa operation in Cheyenne County, Nebraska (Ulysses silt loam and Richfield clay loam mix, AWHC 1.8–2.4 in/ft, groundwater from Ogallala Aquifer at declining rate of 1.8 ft/year, NRD allocation 12.5 acre-inches/year) that must reduce water use 20% while maintaining yields. Design the variable rate irrigation program: (1) soil water monitoring network — install 24 Sentek EnviroSCAN capacitance probes at 4 per pivot circle positioned in high/medium/low AWHC management zones identified by Veris ECa mapping; probe placement at 4, 8, 12, 20-inch depths; data transmitted via cellular to Farmobile dashboard updated every 30 minutes, (2) variable rate irrigation prescription — create 3-zone VRI prescription for each pivot using AquaSpy soil moisture data; sandy Ulysses loam zones: irrigate at 50% plant available water depletion (PAW); clay loam Richfield zones: irrigate at 45% PAW to prevent runoff; average application depth reduced from 1.0 inch to 0.75 inch per irrigation event with zone-specific variation ±0.25 inch, (3) ET-based management — integrate Nebraska High Plains ET Network daily ETo with corn Kc values (0.15 emergence to 1.2 at silking) via AquaSpy cloud platform to automate pivot start/stop based on soil moisture threshold + ET forecast integration, (4) NRD compliance reporting — Lincoln County NRD requires annual water use reporting via Midwest AgEnergy flow meter (±2% accuracy); VRI prescription records serve as documentation for potential water use credit trading at $180/acre-inch, (5) economic analysis — VRI system cost $18,500/pivot (Lindsay FieldNET controller + VRI hardware); annual corn yield maintained at 205 bu/acre on sandy zones (no yield loss from deficit irrigation at 50% PAW) versus 5 bu/acre yield drag risk at 45% PAW on clay zones; water savings 3 acre-inches/year × 2,400 acres × $18/acre-inch NRD value = $129,600/year benefit. Produce a VRI prescription map template and 5-year ROI analysis.","tags":["irrigation","center-pivot","VRI","Nebraska","Ogallala"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-60","title":"Organic Certification Transition Plan for Row Crop and Vegetable Farm","prompt":"You are an organic certification consultant advising a 380-acre farm in Yolo County, California (Rincon silty clay loam, pH 6.9, OM 2.2%) that has grown processing tomatoes and wheat under conventional management and wants to achieve USDA NOP organic certification through California Certified Organic Farmers (CCOF) after completing the 36-month transition period. [Describe your farm situation and current challenges] Design the transition plan: (1) prohibited substance audit — review all inputs applied in the past 3 years; document last synthetic pesticide application (Warrior II lambda-cyhalothrin applied June 2021); confirm 36-month transition clock started July 2021 and certification eligible July 2024; compile field histories and input records for CCOF Organic System Plan submission, (2) weed management transition — replace atrazine and metolachlor with mechanical cultivation (rotary hoe at V1–V2, rolling cultivator at V4); add hairy vetch cover crop winter fallow to suppress summer annuals; flaming with Red Dragon propane flamers at pre-emergence for vegetable beds; budget $28/acre additional cultivation cost versus $14/acre herbicide, (3) fertility transition — replace synthetic 32-0-0 with composted chicken manure (3-3-2) at 4 tons/acre pre-plant for 120 lb N/acre; blood meal (13-0-0) at 200 lb/acre for in-season N supplement; feather meal (12-0-0) as slow-release nitrogen; all inputs verified OMRI-listed before application, (4) insect and disease management — processing tomato IPM transition: Bacillus thuringiensis var. kurstaki (DiPel DF) for tomato fruitworm threshold 10% infested fruit; copper hydroxide (Kocide 3000) for bacterial speck and early blight; Beauveria bassiana (Mycotrol) for western flower thrips, (5) organic premium economics — CCOF certification fee $2,100/year for 380 acres; organic processing tomatoes at $85/ton versus $42/ton conventional; transition year premium available through CCOF Transition Fund at $8/ton above conventional; Year 4 net return improvement projected $180/acre. Produce a year-by-year transition action checklist and organic system plan outline.","tags":["organic","certification","California","transition","CCOF"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-61","title":"Rotational Grazing System for Regenerative Beef on Native Pasture","prompt":"You are a rangeland ecologist and livestock specialist advising a 3,600-acre cow-calf operation in Osage County, Oklahoma (Verdigris silt loam and Bates loam mix, 40-inch annual precipitation, predominantly native tallgrass prairie with big bluestem, little bluestem, and indiangrass) transitioning from continuous season-long grazing to a planned rotational system. Design the complete grazing program: (1) paddock design and water infrastructure — divide 3,200 grazeable acres into 12 paddocks of approximately 267 acres each using permanent 4-strand barbed wire fencing ($1.85/foot installed); install 8 concrete stock tanks (1,500-gallon fiberglass tanks with solar-powered Grundfos pump) fed from 2 existing ponds distributed to provide water access within 0.5 mile for all paddocks at $4,200/tank; (2) stocking and rotation schedule — operate 180 cow-calf pairs (1 AU = 1.4 acres on native tallgrass at 2.7 lb DM/acre/day demand); rotate through all 12 paddocks in 42-day cycle with 3.5 days per paddock in May–September growing season; extend rest periods to 90-day cycle (7.5 days/paddock) in August–September if rainfall < 2 inches; (3) forage monitoring — conduct Robel pole visual obstruction readings at 20 fixed points per paddock before entry and at exit; move when average residual height drops to 8 inches (3,200 lb DM/acre residual) to protect perennial grass root reserves; record entry/exit dates and forage heights in Stockpile app; (4) soil health benchmarks — Haney test annual POXC target: increase from 420 mg/kg baseline to 580 mg/kg by Year 4; soil aggregate stability from 42% to 55%; soil bulk density from 1.48 to 1.38 g/cm³ at 6-inch depth; (5) economic comparison — rotational system capital cost $68,000 (fencing + water); stocking capacity increase from 1 AU/14 acres to 1 AU/11 acres = 38 additional cow-calf pairs = $26,600/year additional gross. Produce a paddock rotation schedule and forage monitoring data sheet.","tags":["regenerative","rotational-grazing","beef","Oklahoma","native-pasture"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-62","title":"Aquaculture System Design for Catfish Pond Production in the Southeast","prompt":"You are an aquaculture specialist with the Mississippi State University Extension Service advising a 480-acre catfish farm in Sunflower County, Mississippi (8 levee ponds averaging 22 acres each, 4-foot average depth) that has been producing channel catfish (Ictalurus punctatus) for 18 years at an average of 3,800 lb/acre/year, selling to Delta Pride Catfish at $0.94/lb. Design a production intensification plan to reach 5,500 lb/acre/year: (1) aeration system upgrade — current paddlewheel aerator capacity is 1.5 HP/acre; upgrade to 3.0 HP/acre using Regal AerMaster Pro 5-HP paddlewheels (2 per 22-acre pond); trigger aeration at dissolved oxygen below 4.0 ppm measured by YSI ProDSS continuous logging sonde; maintain minimum 5 ppm DO for optimal feed conversion; (2) feeding management — increase daily feed rate from 90 lb/acre to 140 lb/acre using Hi-Pro 28% protein floating pellets (Cargill AquaMax Sport Floating); feed twice daily at 7 AM and 4 PM in 30-minute intervals; stop feeding when fish stop surfacing or water temperature exceeds 90°F; automated Sweeney Enterprises belt feeder at $1,200/pond; (3) fingerling stocking — restock at 6,000 fingerlings/acre (7-inch class from Delta Western Hatchery at $0.14/fingerling) in March after winter drawdown; harvest 3-year rotation where oldest class removed at 1.0–1.2 lb market size; maintain mixed-age polyculture with bighead carp at 50/acre for algae control; (4) water quality management — weekly water chemistry testing: total ammonia nitrogen <2.0 ppm, nitrite <0.5 ppm, pH 7.0–8.5, alkalinity 50–150 ppm; apply agricultural limestone at 2 tons/acre if alkalinity below 50 ppm; control aquatic vegetation with triploid grass carp at 10/acre; (5) economics at intensified production — 5,500 lb/acre × 22 acres × 8 ponds = 968,000 lb/year × $0.94 = $909,920/year gross; feed cost increase $48,000/year; aeration upgrade $22,000 capital; net improvement $140,000/year over baseline. Produce an annual production calendar and pond management log template.","tags":["aquaculture","catfish","Mississippi","pond-management","intensification"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-63","title":"Honeybee Colony Management and Honey Production Plan for Diversified Farm","prompt":"You are a master beekeeper and agricultural diversification consultant advising a 250-acre diversified vegetable and orchards operation in Henderson County, North Carolina (Udorthents loam, pH 6.4, 52-inch annual precipitation, strong summer nectar flow from tulip poplar, sourwood, and black locust) wanting to establish 80 beehives to produce premium sourwood honey for direct sale at $18–24/lb. Design the apiary establishment and management plan: (1) colony establishment — purchase 80 3-pound packages with marked queens from Kelley Beeswax Company (mid-April arrival); install in 10-frame Langstroth hive bodies with Pierco plastic foundation; establish 4 apiary sites of 20 hives each at 400-foot minimum spacing to reduce forager competition; each site requires 1-quart entrance feeders with 1:1 sugar syrup for 3 weeks establishment; (2) seasonal management calendar — April: install packages, begin disease monitoring; May–June: add supers when 80% of bottom box drawn; July 1–August 15: primary sourwood flow — no feeding, remove Apivar strips, add 2 honey supers per hive; September: extract honey, apply Apivar Amitraz strips for Varroa control at 2 strips/colony for 8 weeks; December: mite-wash check, feed pollen substitute patties if brood present; (3) Varroa mite management — sample adult bee population using alcohol wash (3% mite per 100 bees = action threshold); apply Apivar (amitraz) September–November at 0.0002 oz active per hive; oxalic acid vapor treatment (Varrox Oxavap at 1.9g/hive) in December for broodless mite kill; target mite load below 1% before spring buildup; (4) honey extraction and marketing — extract in NSF-approved stainless extraction room (2-frame tangential extractor); filter through 600-micron stainless screen; bottle in 1-lb and 2-lb glass bears labeled with Henderson County sourwood origin for premium retail; sell at Asheville City Market and online at $22/lb average; (5) production economics — established colony produces 60 lb sourwood honey/year at peak; 80 hives × 50 lb average = 4,000 lb × $22 = $88,000 gross; operating cost $18,000/year (queens, treatments, equipment); net $70,000/year in Year 3. Produce an annual apiary calendar and queen replacement tracking log.","tags":["beekeeping","honey","North-Carolina","pollinators","specialty-crops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-64","title":"H-2A Agricultural Worker Program Management for Vegetable Farm","prompt":"You are an agricultural labor attorney and HR consultant advising a 320-acre fresh market vegetable operation in Immokalee, Florida (Collier County, Myakka fine sand, pH 6.1) that grows tomatoes, peppers, and cucumbers under 4 Sysco and Fresh Del Monte supply contracts, employing 85 field workers seasonally from November through May. The farm wants to use the H-2A temporary agricultural worker program for the first time to hire 40 certified seasonal workers from Mexico. Design the H-2A program implementation: (1) petition and timeline — file AEWR (Adverse Effect Wage Rate) job order with Florida Department of Economic Opportunity 60 days before workers' start date (October 1 start = file August 1); submit Form I-129H with USCIS at 45-day mark; AEWR for Florida agricultural workers 2024 = $14.53/hour minimum; total wages for 40 workers × 8 months × 40 hours/week = $1,863,000 annual wage commitment; (2) housing requirements — provide housing meeting federal 29 CFR 500.132 standards at no cost to workers; 3-building dormitory at 50 sq ft/person minimum; provide beds, bedding, cooking facilities, hot water, and heating/cooling; Florida OSHA inspection required before worker arrival; annual DOL housing inspection compliance budget $28,000; (3) transportation obligations — provide daily transportation from housing to field at no worker cost using farm-owned 15-passenger vans (DOT commercial driver license required for driver); reimburse inbound travel from Mexico border after 50% of contract completion; outbound travel upon contract completion; (4) three-fourths guarantee — H-2A regulations require guaranteeing 75% of hours in the contract period (8 months × 40 hr/week × 40 workers × 75% = 38,400 hours); document all weather delays and worker availability to defend against guaranteed hours claims; (5) I-9 and E-Verify compliance — complete Form I-9 for all H-2A workers within 3 days of start; enroll in USCIS E-Verify before first petition; retain I-9 records 3 years after hire or 1 year after termination whichever is later. Produce a pre-season H-2A compliance checklist and AEWR wage calculation worksheet.","tags":["farm-labor","H-2A","Florida","immigration-compliance","labor-management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-65","title":"Farm Succession Plan for 1,800-Acre Family Grain Operation","prompt":"You are an agricultural attorney and farm financial advisor helping a 1,800-acre corn-soybean operation in Macon County, Illinois (farmland valued at $11,200/acre = $20.16M total, no debt, annual net income $380,000) plan the transfer from a 72-year-old founder to his daughter (48) who manages daily operations, while fairly compensating his non-farming son (45). The daughter has $180,000 in savings and cannot purchase at market value. [Describe your farm situation and current challenges] Design the succession framework: (1) estate tax calculation and planning — current taxable estate $20.16M land + $2.4M equipment + $1.1M grain + $800K investments = $24.46M; federal estate tax exemption $12.92M (2024); taxable estate $11.54M × 40% = $4.62M federal tax; mitigate with annual gifting ($18,000 × 2 to daughter + 2 to son-in-law = $72,000/year), Irrevocable Life Insurance Trust (ILIT) with $5M policy to provide estate liquidity; (2) special use valuation (Section 2032A) — elect agricultural special use valuation on farmland if actively farmed at death; reduces taxable value from FMV $11,200/acre to $5,400/acre capitalized on rent-to-value ratio; maximum reduction $1.23M (2024 cap), saving $492,000 in estate tax; (3) installment sale structure — father sells 600 acres to daughter at $6.72M financed on 20-year installment note at AFR 4.63%; daughter's annual payment $520,000 funded by farm income; son compensated through life insurance proceeds and retirement account inheritance; (4) entity restructuring — form Illinois agricultural LLC holding all farmland; Class A units (voting, father) and Class B units (non-voting, daughter); gift $18,000/year of Class B units; apply 35% valuation discount for lack of marketability and minority interest; (5) operating transition — implement 3-year management transition: Year 1 father advises, daughter manages; Year 2 daughter sole manager; Year 3 father retires; formalize management compensation at $85,000/year for daughter during transition. Produce a 10-year succession cash flow model and entity structure diagram.","tags":["farm-business","succession-planning","Illinois","estate-planning","family-farm"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-66","title":"USDA FSA Direct Loan Application and Operating Line Planning","prompt":"You are an agricultural lender and farm finance specialist advising a beginning farmer in Brookings County, South Dakota who has farmed rented acres for 4 years and now wants to purchase 320 acres at $7,800/acre ($2.496M) using a USDA FSA Direct Farm Ownership Loan combined with a conventional down payment. The applicant has $180,000 cash, 650 credit score, no land ownership, and $42,000 net farm income last year. [Describe your farm situation and current challenges] Design the financing strategy: (1) FSA Direct Farm Ownership Loan eligibility — beginning farmer definition: has not operated for more than 10 years and does not yet own more than 30% of median farm acreage; South Dakota median farm 487 acres, 30% = 146 acres; applicant qualifies; maximum FSA direct loan $600,000 at 4.625% fixed for 40 years; (2) loan structure — purchase price $2.496M; FSA Direct Ownership: $600,000 (24% of purchase); conventional bank loan: $1.512M at 6.75% (60.5% of purchase); down payment cash: $180,000 + $204,000 seller carry-back note at 5% for 5 years = $384,000 (15.5%); total 100% financing; (3) projected cash flow for loan approval — corn-soybean rotation: 320 acres × average $280/acre net = $89,600 gross; annual debt service: FSA $30,200 + bank $118,000 + seller $46,200 = $194,400; negative $104,800 — requires 220 additional rented acres at $210/acre cash rent to break even; (4) FSA Farm Business Plan requirements — 3-year projected income statements (FINPACK software), balance sheets, cash flow statements; character reference from current landlord; 3 years IRS Schedule F; environmental review completed by FSA; supervised credit requirement — attend approved farm financial management course; (5) USDA Microloan option for operating capital — FSA Microloan (up to $50,000 at 4.125%) for first-year seed, fertilizer, crop insurance = $34,800; simpler application process than standard operating loan; first disbursement within 30 days of approval. Produce a 5-year projected farm income statement and debt service coverage worksheet.","tags":["farm-finance","FSA","beginning-farmer","South-Dakota","agricultural-lending"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-67","title":"USDA MAP Program Application for Specialty Crop Export Development","prompt":"You are an agricultural export marketing specialist advising a cooperative of 14 specialty crop growers in Fresno County, California producing organic almonds, dried apricots, and pistachios wanting to enter the Japanese retail market and expand into South Korea, leveraging the USDA Foreign Agricultural Service Market Access Program (MAP). Design the export development strategy and MAP application: (1) MAP application preparation — eligible organizations: agricultural cooperatives, state departments of agriculture, trade associations; apply through USDA FAS eCPD portal by November 15 for funds effective the following fiscal year; funding request: $450,000 for Year 1 Japan/Korea campaign (MAP typically cost-shares 50% with applicant matching); required match: $450,000 industry funds; total budget $900,000; (2) Japan market entry strategy — Japan is the #3 U.S. tree nut export market at $380M annually; organic almonds have 22% price premium in Japan versus conventional; target Japan Natural Foods Association trade show (Foodex Japan, March) with branded booth presence at $38,000 total cost; in-market broker fees for Delica NZ Ltd. at 8% of FOB value; (3) Korea market development — Korea FTA eliminates tariff on almonds (0%) and pistachios (30% tariff eliminated by 2024 KORUS schedule); partner with Seoul-based importer Lotte Global Logistics for 300 MT trial shipment of organic almonds at $6,200/MT = $1.86M first-year sales target; (4) product certification and labeling — Japan requires JAS organic certification ($4,800 audit) in addition to USDA NOP; Korea requires KOR-GAP food safety equivalency letter from USDA; package in Japanese and Korean language with UPC codes; import health certificates from USDA APHIS; (5) MAP performance reporting — must report sales volume generated, media impressions, and trade show contacts to FAS annually; ROI target: $8 export revenue per $1 MAP investment (FAS benchmark); if Year 1 $900,000 investment generates $7.2M in new export sales, cooperative qualifies for Year 2 continued MAP funding. Produce a MAP budget worksheet and Year 1 marketing activity calendar.","tags":["farm-business","exports","MAP-program","USDA","specialty-crops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-68","title":"Urban Rooftop Farm Design and Economics for Restaurant Supply","prompt":"You are a controlled environment agriculture and urban farming consultant designing a 10,000-square-foot rooftop farm on a 6-story commercial building in Chicago's River North neighborhood (Fulton Market district) for a restaurant group operating 8 farm-to-table restaurants within 3 miles, targeting $120,000/year in direct restaurant sales of microgreens, edible flowers, lettuce, and herbs. Design the rooftop farm system: (1) structural assessment and load calculation — engage structural engineer to verify roof load capacity; shallow growing media systems (4-inch depth Rooflite Intensive Ag blend at 55 lb/sq ft saturated) require minimum 75 psf live load capacity; install 6,000 sq ft of growing beds leaving 4,000 sq ft for pathways, equipment storage, and staff workspace; (2) growing system design — microgreens: 2,400 sq ft of 10×20-inch mesh trays on wire racks (8 tiers high) for 19,200 active trays; LED grow lights (Fluence SPYDRx at 180 µmol/m²/s) on 16-hour photoperiod; 12-day production cycle; 20 varieties including sunflower, radish, pea shoots, and amaranth at $28/lb wholesale average; lettuce and herbs: 3,600 sq ft in NFT trays irrigated from 200-gallon reservoir with Masterblend 4-18-38 nutrient solution; (3) water and energy management — rainwater collection cistern (5,000 gallons, pre-filter, first-flush diverter) supplements municipal water; LED energy use 18,500 kWh/year; total utility cost $6,200/year; in-wall insulation and windscreen for Chicago winter temperature management keeping growing area above 55°F; (4) production capacity and revenue — microgreens 2,400 sq ft × 8 tiers × 12-day cycle × 0.18 lb/tray = 28,800 lb/year × $28 = $806,400 gross (at full capacity); realistic Year 1 at 35% capacity = $282,240; restaurant contract pricing $24–32/lb depending on variety; (5) capital and operating costs — total capital $380,000 (structure reinforcement $85,000, LED systems $140,000, trays/racking $68,000, irrigation $32,000, buildout $55,000); operating $96,000/year; break-even at 42% capacity; Year 3 projected net $86,000. Produce a production schedule by crop and 5-year financial projection.","tags":["urban-farming","vertical-farming","Chicago","rooftop","restaurant-supply"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-69","title":"Community Garden Program Development and Management Plan","prompt":"You are a community food systems planner advising a mid-sized municipality in Minneapolis, Minnesota (Hennepin County) developing a network of 8 community gardens totaling 4.2 acres on city-owned vacant lots in underserved neighborhoods with limited fresh produce access. Design the program framework: (1) site selection and preparation — prioritize parcels in Census tracts with >35% food insecurity rate and no grocery store within 1 mile; conduct Phase I environmental assessment on former industrial lots before soil use; raised bed construction required on contaminated parcels (6-inch depth Cedar raised beds with geotextile liner and Sunshine #3 mix at $2.80/sq ft); install 4-foot perimeter fencing with combination lock for plot holder access; (2) plot allocation system — 20×20-foot individual plots ($45/year fee for residents; fee-waived for SNAP/WIC participants); lottery system with priority points for low-income households (2 points), food insecurity (3 points), and residence in garden neighborhood (1 point); waitlist management through Airtable database; maximum 3-year plot tenure then rotation to waitlist; (3) infrastructure requirements — 100-gallon water storage tank per 10 plots (municipal connection with sub-meter); 8-foot tool storage shed with master lock per site (shovels, rakes, hoses, wheelbarrows); compost bins at 2 per site (Green Johanna 100-gallon bins for plot-holder food scraps); accessible paved pathways to ADA-compliant plots (minimum 10% of plots per ADA standards); (4) programming and education — monthly workshops April–October: seed starting, composting, cover cropping, pest ID, and harvest/preservation; partner with University of Minnesota Extension Master Gardener Program for volunteer instructor support; Somali and Spanish language instruction for underserved populations; youth summer program: 8 plots reserved for school-age participants in partnership with Minneapolis Public Schools; (5) financial sustainability — program budget: $280,000 Year 1 capital + $48,000/year operating; funding: USDA Community Food Projects Competitive Grant ($125,000), plot fees ($6,400/year), City Parks Department $180,000 budget allocation; target 85% self-funding by Year 5 through fees and private grants. Produce a site master plan template and program policy manual outline.","tags":["urban-farming","community-garden","Minneapolis","food-access","program-management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-70","title":"Farmers Market Booth Strategy and Pricing Guide for Vegetable Operation","prompt":"You are an agricultural marketing specialist advising a 12-acre certified organic market garden in Whatcom County, Washington (Everson silt loam, pH 6.5, OM 4.2%) growing 65 vegetable varieties, fruit, and cut flowers that currently sells 40% at Bellingham Farmers Market (Saturday) and 60% through 3 grocery wholesale accounts at 30–40% margin. The grower wants to increase farmers market sales to 70% of revenue. Design the market optimization strategy: (1) booth layout and visual merchandising — rent 20-foot double booth at Bellingham Saturday Market ($42/week, 32 weeks = $1,344); use vertical display (5-tier metal shelving at 7 feet height) to maximize visibility; separate signage by product category with hand-lettered chalkboard price signs; use Pantone 572 green on white brand palette consistently; feature 3 highlighted 'hero products' weekly at eye level (currently best sellers: baby arugula, heirloom tomatoes, cutting-mix flowers); (2) pricing strategy — benchmark against $3–4/head lettuce at Whole Foods; price premium items 40% above wholesale equivalent; bunched herbs $3.50/bunch (versus $1.20 wholesale); flower bouquets $18–24 (material cost $3.80 at 5× markup); implement odd-number pricing ($3.75 not $4.00) shown to increase perceived value; resist discounting at end-of-market — donate excess to NW Harvest to maintain price integrity; (3) customer loyalty and pre-sale system — build email list of 800 regular customers; offer Saturday morning pre-order pickup via Local Line platform at 10% loyalty discount on orders over $30; loyalty card: 10th purchase free encourages repeat visits; CSA hybrid: 6-week summer share $185 sold at market pickup; (4) high-margin product focus — calculate gross margin by crop: salad mix $18.40/lb gross (cost $2.10 to produce), heirloom tomatoes $8.20/lb (cost $1.80), mixed bouquets $14.40/unit (cost $3.20); reallocate 2 acres from low-margin sweet corn ($1,200/acre gross) to salad mix ($22,000/acre gross); (5) market expansion — apply for Bellingham Sunday Depot Market (additional $28/week) for August–October peak season; target Seattle Ballard Farmers Market for Year 3 expansion with $55,000 additional sales potential. Produce a market pricing matrix and weekly sales tracking template.","tags":["direct-marketing","farmers-market","Washington","organic","pricing-strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-71","title":"Soil Microbiome Assessment and Biological Amendment Strategy","prompt":"You are a soil microbiologist and agronomist advising a 640-acre transitioning organic grain farm in Dodge County, Wisconsin (Kewaunee silt loam, pH 7.0, OM 3.6%, 12 years continuous no-till, Haney test biological activity score 12.8) that wants to systematically measure and improve soil biological health to reduce purchased biological inputs and enhance natural nutrient cycling. Design the microbiome assessment and management plan: (1) baseline microbiome characterization — collect 20 soil samples (0–4 inch depth) across management zones at spring sampling; submit to Trace Genomics (San Francisco) for metagenomic 16S rRNA sequencing at $148/sample; analyze: bacterial diversity Shannon index (target >6.0), fungal diversity, Pseudomonas spp. abundance (biocontrol), nitrogen-fixing bacteria (Azospirillum, Azotobacter), mycorrhizal fungal spore density (target >15 spores/gram), nematode food web analysis (bacterivore:fungivore ratio target 3:1); (2) inoculant selection protocol — select biological inoculants based on gap analysis from sequencing: low Trichoderma spp. → apply Trianum-G (Koppert) at 120 g/acre at planting in corn furrow; low Azospirillum → apply Envita (Azospirillum brasilense) at 3.5 fl oz/cwt on corn seed; low rhizobia → reapply HiCoat Ultra (Bradyrhizobium japonicum) on soybean at 5.0 fl oz/cwt seed even in previously inoculated fields; (3) soil carbon substrate management — apply fulvic acid-rich liquid humate (Humic Plus at 1 qt/acre as in-furrow) to feed bacterial communities; ensure minimum 2,000 lb DM/acre cover crop residue to provide microbial substrate; target 55–65% water-filled pore space to optimize aerobic decomposer activity; (4) reducing synthetic input interference — reduce Roundup Ready glyphosate from current 22 oz/acre to 14 oz/acre where IPM allows; avoid metalaxyl seed treatment on fields with high Trichoderma populations (natural damping-off suppression); transition all fungicide use to biological Bacillus amyloliquefaciens (Serenade Opti) on high-OM fields; (5) monitoring outcomes — repeat Trace Genomics sampling Year 2 and Year 4; track POXC (permanganate oxidizable carbon) as sensitive biologically active C indicator; target 15% POXC increase per year; correlate with yield response in paired comparison plots. Produce a soil biology sampling protocol and inoculant decision matrix.","tags":["soil-health","microbiome","biological-amendments","Wisconsin","organic"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-72","title":"Agricultural Water Quality TMDL Compliance Plan for Tile-Drained Watershed","prompt":"You are an agricultural environmental consultant advising a 4-county drainage watershed in northwest Ohio (Blanchard River sub-watershed, tributary to Maumee River, 303(d) listed impaired for total phosphorus and nitrate, subject to Ohio's Maumee River Watershed TMDL requiring 40% phosphorus and 20% nitrate load reduction) where row crop farmers must reduce tile drainage nutrient losses. Design a watershed-scale compliance plan for a 12-farm cooperative totaling 8,400 acres: (1) field-level phosphorus loss assessment — conduct P-Index calculation for all fields using Ohio State University P-Index Calculator: input P soil test (Mehlich-3), application rate, slope, tile drainage presence, buffer width; classify fields as High, Medium, or Low risk; High-P-Index fields (score >150) prohibited from P application until P soil test declines below 150 ppm Mehlich-3; (2) tile drainage water quality monitoring — install AutoSampler (ISCO 3700) and water level flume (Thel-Mar weir box) on outlet tile of 6 representative fields; collect flow-proportional composite samples for NO₃-N and total P weekly May–October; calculate annual load in lb/acre for comparison to TMDL allocations; (3) edge-of-field practice installation — construct drainage water management structures (Agri Drain W-series) on all tile outlets at cost of $820/outlet × 48 outlets = $39,360; apply Ohio H2Ohio program funding at $82/acre for 500 qualifying acres = $41,000 cost-share; install constructed wetlands at 2 watershed low points (8-acre and 4-acre) to remove 35–50% of annual nitrate load from 600 contributing acres; (4) field practice improvements — eliminate broadcast P application in fall on all fields above pH 7.0 (immobile in alkaline conditions); transition to starter P (2×2 placement) at 12 lb P₂O₅/acre; implement 3-year cereal rye cover crop on all tile-drained fields to immobilize fall nitrate and reduce spring tile flow volume 28%; (5) TMDL reporting and credits — submit annual nutrient reduction documentation to Ohio EPA using NRCS RUSLE2 and SNAP-Plus P loss modeling; pursue Ohio EPA certified water quality trading credits at $14/lb P reduction for constructed wetland performance = 1,200 lb P removed/year × $14 = $16,800/year in tradeable credits. Produce a field P-Index map and annual nutrient load monitoring report template.","tags":["water-quality","TMDL","Ohio","tile-drainage","phosphorus"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-73","title":"On-Farm Solar Array Development and Energy Cost Reduction Plan","prompt":"You are an agricultural energy consultant advising a 2,200-acre grain and hog operation in Adair County, Iowa (3,600-head finishing barn consuming 480,000 kWh/year, grain dryers consuming 620,000 kWh/year at $0.098/kWh average blended rate = $108,080/year electric bill) evaluating installation of a 1.2 MW ground-mounted solar array. Design the solar development plan: (1) system design and siting — 1.2 MW single-axis tracking array requires 7.5 acres; site on 8-acre non-productive field corner (waterway edge, irregular shape not suitable for row crops); 3,600 bifacial 335W panels on NEXTracker NX Horizon single-axis trackers; SMA Sunny Tripower Core1 50-kW string inverters (24 inverters); grid interconnection through MidAmerican Energy at 480V 3-phase Point of Delivery; (2) energy production forecast — Iowa average 4.8 peak sun hours/day; 1,200 kW × 4.8 hrs × 365 days × 0.85 performance ratio = 1,785,240 kWh/year generation; covers 1.1M kWh farm use at 100% on-site consumption offset; remaining 685,240 kWh sold at net metering rate $0.065/kWh = $44,540/year; (3) financial analysis — system cost $1.92/W installed = $2.304M total capital; USDA REAP Grant (Rural Energy for America Program): 25% of project cost = $576,000; Federal ITC (Investment Tax Credit): 30% × remaining $1.728M = $518,400; Net investment after incentives: $1,209,600; annual benefit: $108,080 offset + $44,540 sales = $152,620; simple payback 7.9 years; 25-year NPV at 6% = $1.42M; (4) USDA REAP grant application — eligible: agricultural producers and rural small businesses; application submitted to Iowa Rural Development USDA office April 1 deadline; Energy Auditor site visit required ($1,200); 10-year energy production guarantee from installer; (5) agrivoltaics option — consider 100-foot buffer zone between rows planted to native pollinator mix (34 species NRCS prescribed mix) qualifying for additional USDA EQIP pollinator habitat payment at $58/acre. Produce a REAP grant application outline and 25-year cash flow projection.","tags":["farm-energy","solar","Iowa","REAP","agrivoltaics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-74","title":"On-Farm Biogas Digester for Dairy Waste and Energy Production","prompt":"You are an agricultural engineer and renewable energy specialist advising a 1,200-cow dairy operation in Tulare County, California (Fresno County border, CARB Air Quality Management District, existing 8-acre open lagoon emitting 2,100 MT CO₂e/year of methane) that wants to install a covered lagoon anaerobic digester to capture biogas for electricity generation and qualify for California Low Carbon Fuel Standard (LCFS) credits. Design the digester and energy system: (1) covered lagoon design — install 5-acre HDPE geomembrane floating cover over existing lagoon (1.5 mm HDPE, UV-stabilized, $4.20/sq ft installed); biogas collection pipes run along lagoon perimeter to collection header; expected biogas production: 1,200 cows × 15 cubic feet biogas/cow/day = 18,000 cf/day at 60% methane content; (2) biogas utilization — install 65 kW Caterpillar G3304B biogas engine-generator; annual electricity production 65 kW × 8,400 hours × 0.92 availability = 502,320 kWh; sell to Southern California Edison under Rule 21 interconnection at $0.042/kWh avoided cost + PG&E BioMAT at $0.1284/kWh (15-year contract available); heat recovery from engine coolant for facility space heating = $12,000/year propane savings; (3) LCFS credits — California LCFS credits for dairy biogas: CI score −250 gCO₂e/MJ; production volume 18,000 cf/day × 365 × 0.60 CH₄ × 0.0373 MJ/cf = 146,000 MMBtu/year of renewable natural gas equivalent × LCFS credit value; at LCFS credit price $100/MT CO₂e, annual revenue estimated $1.2M/year from LCFS alone; (4) CARB compliance — emission reductions from covered lagoon: 2,100 MT CO₂e/year methane avoidance × 25 GWP = $2.625M CO₂e annual; qualifies for California ARB Compliance Offset Protocol for Livestock Projects; 10-year offset credit contract available; (5) financial summary — total project cost $2.8M; USDA REAP grant 25% = $700,000; federal ITC 30% = $630,000; net capital $1.47M; annual revenue: electricity $64,800 + LCFS $1.2M + heat savings $12,000 = $1.276M; payback 1.15 years. Produce a project permitting timeline and financial model.","tags":["farm-energy","biogas","digester","California","dairy-waste"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-75","title":"EQIP Application and Practice Implementation for Soil Health Program","prompt":"You are a USDA NRCS conservation planner advising a 780-acre corn-soybean operation in Saline County, Nebraska (Hastings silt loam, pH 7.1, OM 2.8%, current tillage: fall chisel plow + spring field cultivator, no cover crops, tile drainage on 320 acres) applying for USDA EQIP (Environmental Quality Incentives Program) funding to implement a soil health system. Design the application and practice plan: (1) EQIP application strategy — Nebraska NRCS signup opens March 1; apply for Conservation Stewardship Program (CSP) enhancement bundle: No-Till (342), Cover Crop (340), Nutrient Management Plan (590), and Drainage Water Management (554); prioritize application with ranking criteria: highly erodible land (HEL) fields score highest, fields in Saline River watershed (water quality priority area), beginning farmer status (+5 ranking points); (2) practice payment rates for Nebraska 2024 — Cover Crop practice 340: $38/acre; No-Till practice 342: $28/acre; Drainage Water Management 554: $82/outlet structure (48 outlets = $3,936); Nutrient Management Plan 590: $8.50/acre; total EQIP payment 780 acres × $66/acre average + structures = $55,116/year for 5-year contract = $275,580 total; (3) nutrient management plan development — hire certified crop adviser (CCA) to develop NRCS 590 Nutrient Management Plan: soil sample all fields at 2.5-acre grid; develop N, P, K, and S recommendations with agronomic maximum N rates (setback from drainages); plan submitted to NRCS office before field work authorization; CCA fee $18/acre for plan = $14,040; (4) cover crop establishment plan — aerial seed cereal rye at 60 lb/acre into standing corn at V4 stage via High Plains Cooperative aerial application at $12/acre; second-year add hairy vetch at 8 lb/acre to rye mix for nitrogen fixation credit; maintain cover crop records (seeding date, rates, termination dates) for EQIP payment verification; (5) CSP upgrade from EQIP — after 5-year EQIP contract, apply for Conservation Stewardship Program at enhanced payment rate: $18–$22/acre/year for maintaining no-till, diverse cover crop, and nutrient management system simultaneously. Produce an EQIP application timeline and practice implementation calendar.","tags":["USDA-conservation","EQIP","Nebraska","soil-health","conservation-planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-76","title":"Conservation Stewardship Program Enhancement for Diverse Rotation Farm","prompt":"You are a USDA NRCS conservation agronomist advising a 920-acre diversified grain and hay operation in Douglas County, Kansas (Woodson silt loam, pH 6.3, OM 2.9%, 4-crop rotation: corn, soybean, winter wheat, grain sorghum) that is 2 years into a 5-year EQIP contract and wants to upgrade to Conservation Stewardship Program (CSP) at contract renewal for higher annual payments. Design the CSP upgrade plan: (1) CSP eligibility and payment structure — CSP pays $18–$22/acre/year (versus EQIP $12–15/acre/year) for farms demonstrating high conservation performance on existing practices plus adding 2 new enhancement activities per year; current EQIP practices (cover crop, reduced till, nutrient management plan) count as existing performance; payment = base rate $9.60/acre + enhancement payments averaging $11.50/acre for 920 acres = $19,320/year; (2) enhancement activity selection — select 6 enhancement activities from NRCS activity menu targeting maximum payment: E328A Soil Health Monitoring ($3.20/acre, 920 acres), E340F Adaptive Nutrient Management ($4.80/acre), E328D Diverse Cover Crop Cocktail Mix ($3.60/acre), E380A Prescribed Grazing on Cropland Aftermath ($2.10/acre), E333B Hedgerow Planting ($280/100 LF), E645A Pollinator Habitat Strip ($4.40/acre on 20 acres); total enhancement payments $15,620/year; (3) soil health monitoring requirement — CSP E328A requires annual Haney soil health scoring: collect 10 samples/field annually; submit to Ward Laboratories for Haney/Solvita test at $32/sample; document POXC improvement baseline to target (680 mg/kg to 820 mg/kg over 5-year period); (4) pollinator habitat installation — establish 2-acre strips at field edges using NRCS CP-42 native wildflower mix (12 species: coneflower, black-eyed Susan, wild bergamot, prairie blazing star); seeding at 20 lb PLS/acre in 20-foot wide strips along 3,200 linear feet of field margins; (5) 5-year financial summary — CSP total: $19,320/year × 5 years = $96,600; compared to EQIP renewal: $12,800/year × 5 = $64,000; CSP premium $32,600 additional over 5 years. Produce a CSP enhancement activity selection matrix and payment calculation worksheet.","tags":["USDA-conservation","CSP","Kansas","soil-health","conservation-planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-77","title":"CRP Enrollment and Waterfowl Habitat Enhancement for Prairie Pothole Farm","prompt":"You are a USDA Farm Service Agency conservation planner advising a 1,600-acre diversified grain farm in Day County, South Dakota (Prairie Pothole Region, 68 scattered wetland potholes totaling 94 acres, Swenoda loam uplands surrounding potholes, currently cropland) where the landowner wants to enroll the wetland margins and buffer acres in Conservation Reserve Program (CRP) for annual rental payments and wildlife habitat. Design the CRP enrollment plan: (1) practice eligibility assessment — 94 acres of seasonal and temporary wetland (Type III and IV basins per Cowardin classification) are eligible for CP23 Wetland Restoration with adjacent buffer; identify 140 additional upland buffer acres within 100 feet of wetland margins for CP9 Shallow Water for Wildlife; total enrollment potential 234 acres; (2) rental rate calculation — South Dakota Day County CRP rental rates: cropland acres $96/acre × 234 acres = $22,464/year; wetland acres qualify for USDA CRP Wetland Restoration CP-23 at 1.5× rental rate = $144/acre; 94 wetland acres × $144 = $13,536; upland buffer 140 acres × $96 = $13,440; total annual CRP rental $26,976 for 10-year contract = $269,760; (3) wetland restoration work — 24 of 68 basins have tile drainage that must be removed or controlled; remove 2,800 feet of tile (JI Case backhoe at $8.50/foot = $23,800); install water control structures on 6 primary basins; replant upland buffer to native grass (NRCS CP25 mix: western wheatgrass, green needlegrass, blue grama at 8 lb PLS/acre); (4) wildlife co-benefits and hunting lease income — restored prairie potholes produce 2.4 duck broods/basin/year (USFWS Central Flyway average); 68 potholes × 2.4 broods = 163 duck broods; lease hunting rights to private hunting club at $22/acre/year on 600-acre block = $13,200/year hunting income; qualify for NRCS Wildlife Habitat Incentives Program for additional $28/acre wetland management payment; (5) CRP signup timing — general CRP signup occurs annually in early February; continuous CRP signup (wetland practices) available year-round; submit FSA-848 application with aerial photo, Conservation Plan from NRCS, and FSA Farm Records to county FSA office. Produce a wetland basin restoration prioritization map and 10-year CRP financial analysis.","tags":["USDA-conservation","CRP","South-Dakota","wetlands","wildlife-habitat"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-78","title":"Agricultural Drone Application Program for Fungicide and Foliar Spraying","prompt":"You are a precision agriculture and drone operations specialist advising a 4,800-acre corn and soybean operation in Cass County, Missouri (flat to gently rolling terrain, average field size 68 acres) that wants to add drone aerial application capability for fungicide timing flexibility versus ground rig limitations in wet springs when fields are too wet for 65,000-lb sprayer entry. Design the drone spray program: (1) equipment selection and FAA compliance — purchase DJI Agras T40 (40-liter tank, 8 rotors, 3.26 gal/min flow rate, 12 m effective swath width) at $17,800; obtain FAA Part 137 aerial application certificate (separate from Part 107) — requires 250 hours fixed-wing OR 100 hours rotary experience; register with FAA UAS registry; Missouri Department of Agriculture pesticide applicator license for aerial application; (2) flight planning for corn fungicide — target VT through R1 growth stage for Quilt Xcel application (azoxystrobin + propiconazole at 14 fl oz/acre); DJI Agras T40 sprays 50 acres/hour at 7 mph and 2 m altitude; spray 200 acres/day with 2 flights and battery swap cycle; 10-hour operation window; full 4,800 acres requires 24 days — prioritize 1,600 high-yield acres first; (3) calibration and application accuracy — calibrate T40 flow rate at 2 gallons/acre (GPA) using collection containers at 5 points across spray swath; verify ±5% uniformity coefficient; use DJI Agras ground station mission planning at 3-meter swath overlap; set boom height at 2m above canopy for Quilt Xcel minimum efficacy height; (4) product handling and mixer setup — T40 tank fill station with 100-gallon nurse tank on ATV; dissolve wettable powder formulations completely before loading; rinse procedure (triple-rinse with deionized water before product switch); maintain MSDS binder and pesticide use records for Missouri MDOA inspection; (5) economics — drone application cost: $18.50/acre (amortized equipment + fuel + labor) versus $12.80/acre ground rig; drone premium justified when field timing value exceeds $5.70/acre difference: corn fungicide at VT = 4–6 bu/acre yield benefit × $5.00 = $20–30/acre value at risk. Produce a drone spray operation SOP and flight mission planning template.","tags":["precision-ag","drones","fungicide","Missouri","aerial-application"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-79","title":"Telematics and Fleet Management System for Large Farm Equipment","prompt":"You are a farm operations technology consultant advising a 5,400-acre corn-soybean operation in Cass County, Iowa (4 tractors, 2 combines, 3 grain carts, 2 planting units, 1 sprayer — 12 total machines) implementing a telematics platform to reduce $28,000/year in unnecessary idle time and improve maintenance compliance. Design the telematics program: (1) platform selection — evaluate John Deere Operations Center (free with JD equipment but limited cross-brand functionality), CNH Industrial FleetLink ($180/machine/year), and Trimble Ag Connected Farm ($220/machine/year); Case IH AFS Connect free telematics included on Magnum 340 CVT and Puma 185; recommend integrating Samsara AG-series OBD II trackers at $35/month on non-JD legacy equipment for unified fleet dashboard; (2) idle time reduction — baseline: 12 machines averaging 2.8 hours/day idle out of 10-hour operating day = 28% idle rate; target 18% (1.8 hours/day); program Samsara to generate alert if idle exceeds 20 consecutive minutes; monthly idle time report by operator and machine; at $11.20/hour idle fuel cost × 200 hours saved × 12 machines = $26,880/year potential savings; (3) preventive maintenance scheduling — auto-generate work orders at engine hour thresholds: oil change every 250 hours (Samsara alert at 230 hours), filter replacement every 500 hours, combine concave adjustment check at 1,000-hour intervals; track parts inventory in Samsara maintenance module; calculate true cost of deferred maintenance (combine head breakdown during harvest = $8,000/day downtime at custom rate cost); (4) fuel consumption benchmarking — telematics fuel flowmeter data shows Magnum 340 consuming 6.2 gal/hour at tillage versus John Deere benchmark 5.8 gal/hour for equivalent 300 HP tillage operation; diagnose: worn injector set confirmed by engine diagnostic code P0201; repair cost $1,800 versus $8,400 annual fuel waste; (5) operator performance reporting — monthly operator scorecard: idle time %, excessive speed events (>12 mph on headlands), harsh braking events; share scores in team meeting; top-performer bonus $500/quarter; privacy policy — operators notified of monitoring in employment agreement. Produce a fleet telematics implementation checklist and monthly dashboard KPI template.","tags":["precision-ag","telematics","fleet-management","Iowa","farm-operations"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-80","title":"GAP Certification Preparation for Direct-to-Retail Fresh Vegetable Farm","prompt":"You are a food safety consultant advising a 68-acre fresh vegetable operation in Stanislaus County, California (Hanford sandy loam, pH 7.2) growing tomatoes, bell peppers, and cantaloupe under a Safeway direct-to-store purchase agreement that requires Primus GFS v3 food safety certification at a minimum score of 90% by next season. [Describe your farm situation and current challenges] Design the GAP certification preparation program: (1) pre-assessment gap analysis — conduct internal mock audit using Primus GFS v3 audit checklist (430 questions across 7 modules: Management System, Worker Health and Hygiene, Land History and Adjacent Land Use, Soil Amendments, Irrigation Water, Pesticide Use, and Harvest Equipment); prioritize corrective actions for Critical Deficiency items (automatic fail) versus Major findings (5-point deduction each); (2) worker health and hygiene infrastructure — install OSHA-compliant hand washing stations: 1 unit per 20 workers within 200 feet; stations must include running water, liquid soap in dispenser, single-use paper towels, and covered waste can; add permanent restroom facilities at $12,800 per 2-stall unit (county permit required); develop written illness and injury policy with symptom reporting forms in Spanish and English; (3) water testing program — test all irrigation water sources quarterly for generic E. coli (EPA Method 1603); surface canal water (EC San Joaquin District No. 8) requires 5-sample geometric mean ≤126 CFU/100mL for overhead irrigation; maintain water quality log with lab certificates; identify 120-day and 0-day pre-harvest intervals by application method; (4) pesticide and soil amendment documentation — compile 2-year pesticide application records with operator license numbers, application dates, rates, REI compliance; document all soil amendments (compost temperature logs, biosolids application restrictions within 4 months of harvest); (5) audit scheduling and certification maintenance — schedule Primus GFS audit through Food Safety Net Services ($1,800 audit fee); allow 6-week corrective action window before re-audit; annual re-certification required; designate food safety manager (minimum 4-hour FSMA Produce Safety Alliance training) as internal audit lead. Produce a 90-day pre-audit corrective action plan and documentation checklist.","tags":["food-safety","GAP","California","Primus","retail-produce"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-81","title":"FSMA Preventive Controls Plan for On-Farm Value-Added Processing","prompt":"You are a food safety regulatory specialist advising a 220-acre apple orchard and cider operation in Okanogan County, Washington that produces 18,000 gallons of pasteurized apple cider annually and 4,200 gallons of unpasteurized cider under the Washington State direct farm sales exemption. The operation's gross food sales exceed $1M, triggering FSMA Preventive Controls for Human Food (PCHF) registration requirements. Design the FSMA compliance system: (1) registered food facility status — register with FDA as a food facility (Form 3537) by October 1; biennial renewal; domestic facility registration triggers FSMA jurisdiction for pasteurized cider; designate a Preventive Controls Qualified Individual (PCQI) — FSPCA PCQI training course (2-day, $595) required for the food safety plan owner; (2) hazard analysis — identify biological hazards: Salmonella and E. coli O157:H7 (environmental contamination of fallen fruit); chemical hazards: patulin mycotoxin in moldy apple culls (FDA action level 50 ppb in juice); physical hazards: stem and seed fragments in cider; document hazard evaluation in written hazard analysis per 21 CFR 117.130; (3) preventive controls for pasteurized cider — Critical Control Point: flash pasteurization at 160°F for 6 seconds (validated kill step for 5-log E. coli reduction); monitor via continuous thermocouple with chart recorder (paper audit trail); corrective action: re-pasteurize or destroy product if temperature record shows deviation; (4) supply chain preventive controls — require Certificate of Analysis from glass bottle supplier confirming lead content <0.1 ppm; Qualified Supplier Approval (QSA) records for pectin and ascorbic acid ingredients; annual supplier audit or third-party certificate verification; (5) food safety plan review and records — annual review by PCQI of all preventive controls and monitoring procedures; retain monitoring records 2 years minimum; environmental monitoring swab records (Listeria indicator testing) 2-year retention; facility available for FDA inspection within 24 hours of request. Produce a food safety plan template and hazard analysis worksheet.","tags":["food-safety","FSMA","value-added","Washington","cider"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-82","title":"Trout Aquaculture System Design for Cold-Water Stream Farm","prompt":"You are an aquaculture engineer and fisheries specialist advising a 180-acre farm in Bath County, Virginia (Cowpasture River watershed, natural spring water source at 52°F year-round, 400 GPM flow from limestone karst spring) that wants to establish a rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) raceway production system to supply Virginia restaurant markets at $8.50/lb live weight. Design the production system: (1) raceway design — construct 6 concrete raceways (100 feet × 10 feet × 4 feet deep = 4,000 gallons each) in series using gravity flow from spring; water flows sequentially through raceways at 3 acre-feet/day total; inlet flow 400 GPM through a 6-inch PVC gravity main; slope 1% for self-cleaning; install concrete sump at end of each raceway with 3-inch drain for solids removal; (2) stocking and production schedule — stock 8,000 fingerlings per raceway (12,000/acre-foot equivalent); obtain 3-inch fingerlings (60/lb) from Hatchery Creek Farms, Swoope, VA at $0.42/fingerling; grow to 1.0 lb market weight in 14–16 months at 55°F water temperature; feed rate: 1.5% body weight/day with Zeigler Trophy Trout 3.5mm pellet (44% protein, 14% fat); FCR 1.1 lb feed per lb gain; (3) water quality management — dissolved oxygen maintained above 8.0 ppm by LiqO2 liquid oxygen injection diffuser at each raceway inlet; ammonia-N monitored weekly with Hach colorimetric test kit; target TAN <1.0 ppm; flow-through system naturally dilutes metabolites; conduct annual benthic macroinvertebrate survey downstream for VPDES permit compliance; (4) VPDES permit compliance — Virginia NPDES general permit VAG81 for aquaculture facilities <10 MGD; effluent monitoring: total suspended solids <45 mg/L, pH 6–9, settleable solids <0.5 mL/L; install settling pond (0.25 acre, 4-foot depth) at system outlet to capture 80% of settleable solids before discharge to stream; (5) marketing plan — 6 raceways × 8,000 fish × 1.0 lb average = 48,000 lb/year at $8.50/lb = $408,000/year gross; direct distribution to 12 Richmond and Charlottesville restaurants at twice-weekly live delivery in 150-gallon aerated transport tanks; Virginia Department of Health aquaculture dealer license required. Produce a raceway management calendar and water quality monitoring log.","tags":["aquaculture","trout","Virginia","raceway","water-quality"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-83","title":"Commercial Beekeeping Operation Expansion and Pollination Services","prompt":"You are a commercial beekeeping specialist advising a 280-colony Midwestern beekeeping operation in Ingham County, Michigan currently producing $0.82/lb wildflower honey sold wholesale, wanting to add pollination service contracts with Michigan blueberry and cherry orchards to double annual revenue. Design the expansion plan: (1) colony strength requirements for pollination contracts — Michigan blueberry growers require minimum 8 frames of bees (4-frame equivalent) per colony; MSU Extension standard: 2 colonies/acre for highbush blueberry pollination; target 80-acre orchard contracts = 160 colonies required; build from 280 to 400 colonies over 18 months by purchasing 3-lb packages at $145/package in April and splitting strong colonies at 60% split success rate; (2) pollination contract pricing — Michigan blueberry pollination market rate: $160–$195/colony for 4-week placement (May 5–June 1); cherry pollination $140–$165/colony for 2-week placement (April 20–May 5); target 200 colonies for blueberry × $175/colony = $35,000; 100 colonies for cherry × $150 = $15,000; total pollination gross $50,000 versus current honey revenue $22,400/year (280 colonies × 10 lb average × $0.82); (3) colony management for Michigan winters — Michigan overwinter colony losses average 28%; reduce losses to 18% with 3-year varroa management program: Apivar treatment September (post-honey harvest), oxalic acid treatment December (broodless period), formic acid (Miteaway Quick Strips) July if mite count exceeds 2%; maintain winter cluster population above 5 frames by protecting with insulated Bee Cozy winter wraps; (4) pollination placement logistics — deliver colonies in April at night using 24-foot flatbed trailer with ratchet straps; GPS-plot hive locations in FieldMaps ArcGIS for landowner record; placement within 300 feet of blueberry block perimeter fence; provide written contract with payment due upon colony placement, 30-day residue clause; (5) honey production from pollination sites — colonies placed in blueberry orchards return 15–22 lb white honey premium; target blueberry blossom honey at $1.80/lb retail = $126,000 additional retail honey revenue; direct sale at Ann Arbor Farmers Market at $14/lb for specialty honey. Produce a colony inventory growth plan and pollination contract template.","tags":["beekeeping","pollination","Michigan","commercial-apiary","blueberry"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-84","title":"Farm Labor Compliance Audit and Wage Theft Prevention Program","prompt":"You are an agricultural labor law specialist and HR consultant advising a 650-acre diversified vegetable operation in Monterey County, California employing 120 permanent and seasonal workers across lettuce, broccoli, and strawberry harvesting and packing. The operation received a California Labor Commissioner citation last year for $84,000 in unpaid overtime and piece-rate calculation errors. Design a comprehensive labor compliance system: (1) piece-rate compliance — California Labor Code §226.2 requires paying piece-rate workers a separate minimum wage for rest period time and non-productive time (tool sharpening, walking to field, waiting for crop readiness); calculate separately: rest period pay = state minimum wage × 10 minutes per 4-hour period per worker; non-productive time tracked by supervisor timesheet; all piece-rate workers must receive itemized wage statements showing piece-rate units, applicable piece rate, and separately listed rest/non-productive time compensation; (2) overtime calculation for agricultural workers — California agricultural overtime (AB 1066 fully implemented 2022): overtime after 8 hours/day and 40 hours/week at 1.5×; double time after 12 hours/day; track hours using Kronos Workforce Central biometric time clocks at each field entry point ($8,400 for 4 units); integrate with Paycom payroll for automated OT calculation; (3) workers' compensation program — California agricultural workers' comp premium for Monterey County vegetable: $14.80 per $100 payroll (WCIRB rate); reduce rate through State Fund safety dividend program requiring documented safety training, incident investigation within 24 hours, and light-duty return-to-work policy; target experience modifier (X-Mod) below 0.85; (4) housing compliance for resident farmworkers — California Health and Safety Code §17021.5 labor camp inspection: maintain 50 sq ft/person minimum; fire extinguisher inspection quarterly; potable water testing monthly (coliform: 0 CFU/100mL); smoke detector in every sleeping room; Cal/OSHA housing inspection record maintained on-site; (5) mandatory AB 1825 and SB 1343 training — California requires 2-hour harassment prevention training for supervisors every 2 years; 1-hour training for all non-supervisors; training in Spanish required; document completion in employee file. Produce a labor compliance calendar and piece-rate pay calculation template.","tags":["farm-labor","labor-compliance","California","wages","HR"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-85","title":"Mycorrhizal Fungi and Biostimulant Program for Corn Production","prompt":"You are a soil biologist and agronomist advising a 740-acre corn-soybean operation in Kossuth County, Iowa (Nicollet silty clay loam, pH 6.7, OM 3.8%, no-till 6 years, Haney test biological score 14.2) interested in reducing synthetic fertilizer use 15% by optimizing the mycorrhizal fungal network and implementing a comprehensive biostimulant program. Design the biological management system: (1) mycorrhizal community assessment — collect 15 soil samples at corn-soybean rotation stages; send to Mycorrhizal Applications for spore density analysis and root colonization assessment (25 root samples per field); baseline: Glomus intraradices colonization rate 42%; target 65% colonization by Year 3; avoid phosphorus application above 80 lb P₂O₅/acre to prevent mycorrhizal suppression; (2) in-furrow biostimulant program — apply Myco Bliss granular mycorrhizal inoculant (Glomus intraradices + G. mosseae at 4 species) at 2 lb/acre in corn planter furrow via Precision Planting CleanSweep attachment; combine with humic acid (Humic DG at 10 lb/acre) and kelp-based seaweed extract (Kelpak at 0.5 qt/acre) for synergistic root growth stimulation; expected ROI: 4.2 bu/acre corn yield increase at $22.50/acre total cost at $5.00/bu corn = net $21/acre benefit; (3) nitrogen biofixation supplementation — apply Envita (Azospirillum brasilense GA200) at 3.5 fl oz/cwt corn seed treating; documented 24–40 lb N/acre fixation in Iowa State University trials; reduce starter N application from 30 lb N/acre to 15 lb N/acre when Envita applied, saving $7.20/acre at $0.48/lb N; maintain 10-acre untreated check strip for comparison; (4) phosphorus solubilizing bacteria — apply Phospho-Relief (Bacillus megaterium) in-furrow at 4 oz/acre; solubilizes unavailable soil P from legacy P buildup (soil P 48 ppm Bray P1); reduces P fertilizer requirement by 15 lb P₂O₅/acre on high-P fields = $6.30/acre savings; (5) fungicide compatibility — conventional metalaxyl + fludioxonil seed treatments (Lumivia) reduce Azospirillum survival 68% on seed; switch to biological seed treatment Vault HP (Bradyrhizobium + rhizobia) on soybeans and Vibrance (fludioxonil only) on corn to preserve biostimulant viability. Produce a biostimulant program cost-benefit worksheet and field comparison trial design.","tags":["soil-health","mycorrhizal","biostimulants","Iowa","biological-amendments"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-86","title":"Soil Health Benchmark Program Using Haney Test Across Diverse Rotations","prompt":"You are a soil health specialist and agronomist advising a 1,200-acre diversified operation in Bartholomew County, Indiana (Crosby silt loam and Brookston silty clay loam, pH 6.5–7.1, OM 2.8–3.6%) with 4 management zones: conventional corn-soybean tillage (Zone 1), no-till corn-soybean (Zone 2), no-till with cover crops (Zone 3), and certified organic vegetable production (Zone 4). Design a systematic soil health benchmark program: (1) Haney soil health test protocol — collect 20 samples per zone at 0–6 inch depth in April; submit to Ward Laboratories for complete Haney/Solvita test: POXC, water extractable organic C and N, respiration CO₂ burst, Solvita CO₂ score, total organic N, and Haney economic index; calculate zone-level baselines for 3-year trend analysis; expected scores: Zone 1 (conventional): 4.2; Zone 2 (no-till): 8.6; Zone 3 (no-till + cover crop): 14.8; Zone 4 (organic): 18.4; (2) POXC (permanganate oxidizable carbon) as active carbon indicator — POXC reflects the biologically active carbon fraction; Zone 1 baseline 320 mg/kg versus Zone 4 baseline 820 mg/kg; target POXC increase of 40 mg/kg/year in all zones through cover crop addition; correlate POXC with nitrogen mineralization rate (regression target R² > 0.68) to reduce synthetic N application rates; (3) soil protein index — use Haney soil N test to predict N mineralization: soil protein index (SPI) of 6.0 = 24 lb N/acre available in-season; reduce corn pre-plant N application by 0.8 lb N per SPI unit above 4.0; Zone 3 with SPI 8.2 = (8.2-4.0) × 0.8 = 3.4 lb N/acre credit = 3.4 lb/acre N reduction = $1.63/acre fertilizer savings; (4) aggregate stability as compaction proxy — measure wet aggregate stability (Yoder wet-sieve method) annually in each zone at 0–4 inch; Zone 1 baseline 38% macroaggregates > 0.25 mm; Zone 4 baseline 74%; track improvement toward Zone 3 target 62% in 5 years through cover crop and reduced tillage; (5) economic soil health score — Haney economic index: Zone 3 score 14.8 → N reduction 28 lb/acre savings + 2 bu/acre yield premium = $13.30 + $9.50 = $22.80/acre benefit versus $3.80/acre testing cost. Produce a multi-zone soil health scorecard and 5-year trend tracking spreadsheet.","tags":["soil-health","Haney-test","benchmark","Indiana","biological-fertility"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-87","title":"Farmland Cash Rent Negotiation Strategy for Expanding Corn-Soybean Farm","prompt":"You are an agricultural economist and farm business consultant advising a 1,400-acre corn-soybean farm in Boone County, Iowa that rents 840 acres from 6 different landlords at an average $268/acre — above the county average of $248/acre — and is losing $29,800/year in excess rent relative to market. The farm operator wants a systematic approach to lease renegotiation at upcoming December renewals. Design the negotiation strategy: (1) market data assembly — download Iowa State University Extension Land Value Survey and Iowa Farmland Owners Survey for Boone County 2024; compile ISU Extension Cash Rental Rates for Iowa report (county average $248/acre, range $215–$310); pull USDA NASS Land Values data for Central Iowa CRD; document each rented parcel's CSR2 (Corn Suitability Rating) — target 5–8% rent reduction on parcels with CSR2 below 80 (below county average productivity); (2) CSR2-based rent calculation — Iowa benchmark rent formula: Annual Rent = CSR2 × multiplier; current Boone County multiplier $3.40/CSR2 point; parcel with CSR2 72 × $3.40 = $244.80/acre fair rent versus current $275/acre = $30.20/acre overcharge × 180 acres = $5,436/year savings target on that parcel; (3) flex rent proposal — propose Flex Lease to 3 willing landlords: base rent $220/acre plus profit-sharing clause when corn cash price × yield > $1,050/acre gross trigger; cap flex payment at $40/acre above base; landlord shares in upside while operator has downside protection; present historical flex lease data showing landlords received $242/acre average over 5 years versus $248 fixed (slightly below market but with upside participation); (4) relationship management — begin lease discussions September 1 (90 days before December 1 renewal deadline); document any drainage tile repairs, liming, or cover crop establishment made on each parcel as goodwill value; offer multi-year lease (3 years) with annual rent index adjustment = landlord gets stability in exchange for slight discount; (5) alternative tenant risk — research 3 neighboring operators who might bid on released ground; if CSR2-justified market rent is $244 and landlord demands $278, calculate true break-even on parcel before walking away. Produce a lease comparison worksheet and negotiation talking points by landlord.","tags":["farm-business","cash-rent","Iowa","lease-negotiation","farmland"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-88","title":"Direct-to-Consumer Pork Marketing Program for Pasture-Raised Operation","prompt":"You are an agricultural marketing specialist advising a 280-acre pasture-raised pork operation in Polk County, Iowa raising 360 Berkshire-Duroc crossbred pigs annually on rotational grazing paddocks, currently selling all live animals to a conventional packer at $65/cwt live weight. The operation wants to capture direct-market value at $8–12/lb for pasture-raised pork. Design the marketing program: (1) USDA custom-exempt versus inspected processing — USDA custom-exempt processing (state-licensed) permits only direct farm sales, CSA, and on-farm sales (not retail or wholesale); USDA-inspected processing required for farmers market, restaurant, and grocery sales; locate USDA-inspected processor within 120 miles — closest Iowa USDA pork facility: Lorentz Meats, Cannon Falls, MN (200 miles) or local USDA small plant; kill fee $180/head + cut-and-wrap $0.85/lb carcass weight; (2) product mix and pricing — average Berkshire hog: 260 lb live × 73% dressing = 190 lb hanging weight × 73% cutting yield = 138 lb retail cuts; whole hog at $8.50/lb HCW = $1,615/head versus conventional $169/head; half-hog package $875 (92 lb HCW × $9.50); quarter shares for single families $480; individual cuts: chops $14/lb, roasts $10/lb, sausage $9/lb; (3) marketing channels — primary: direct online store via FarmDrop platform at $125/year subscription; email list of 620 past customers from 2-year farmers market presence; target 240 whole/half hog sales annually = 120 whole + 240 half = absorbs 240 animals (67% of production); remaining 120 animals remain conventional; (4) cold chain and storage — invest in 8×12 walk-in freezer ($14,200 installed) for 1,500 lb storage capacity; coordinate with processor for 4 bi-monthly kill dates (90 animals per kill) to maintain inventory; offer free delivery within 25-mile radius on orders over $200; (5) financial comparison — direct market 240 head × $1,200 average net/head = $288,000; conventional 120 head × $169/head = $20,280; total gross $308,280 versus all-conventional $60,840. Produce a production and marketing calendar and break-even analysis by sales channel.","tags":["direct-marketing","pork","Iowa","pasture-raised","direct-to-consumer"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-89","title":"Irrigation Water Rights Acquisition and TMDL Management Plan","prompt":"You are an agricultural water attorney and environmental compliance specialist advising a 1,600-acre specialty vegetable operation in Weld County, Colorado (South Platte River basin, irrigated with Colorado Big Thompson (CBT) water units and direct-flow priority rights dating to 1914) that wants to acquire 80 additional CBT units to support expansion and must comply with the South Platte River TMDL for selenium and salinity. Design the water rights acquisition and TMDL compliance plan: (1) CBT unit acquisition — current market price $65,000–$75,000/CBT unit; purchase 80 units × $70,000 = $5.6M acquisition cost; finance through Colorado Agricultural Finance Authority (CAFA) water loan at 4.85% for 20 years = $439,000/year debt service; each CBT unit delivers average 0.7 acre-feet/year; 80 units × 0.7 = 56 additional acre-feet for 80 new irrigated acres at 0.7 AF/acre/year; (2) water efficiency upgrade for existing system — current irrigation efficiency 72% on 1,200 acres; upgrade 400 acres from furrow to subsurface drip (SDI) at 92% efficiency; SDI saves 0.35 AF/acre = 140 AF/year reduction; banked water with Northern Colorado Water Conservancy District for lease to downstream users at $180/AF = $25,200/year lease income; (3) selenium TMDL compliance — South Platte selenium TMDL (Colorado WQCD) requires farm-level load allocation; selenium source: natural seleniferous Niobrara shale soils (soil Se 1.8–4.2 mg/kg); management practice: gypsum application reduces soil Se bioavailability 35%; deficit irrigation in late summer reduces selenium leaching by 28%; annual third-party selenium monitoring in tile drainage (target <0.05 mg/L); (4) Colorado CDPHE agricultural return flow permit — obtain State Engineer's Office agricultural return flow permit for tile drainage discharge to Galeton Lateral; comply with Regulation 85 selenium standards through farm-level selenium management plan approved by CDPHE within 90 days; (5) Section 404 wetland mitigation — SDI pipeline installation requires Nationwide Permit 12 from USACE for 3 wetland crossings; purchase 2.4 acres of wetland mitigation credits from Front Range Wetlands Mitigation Bank at $12,400/acre = $29,760. Produce a water rights acquisition timeline and selenium load reduction model.","tags":["water-rights","TMDL","Colorado","irrigation","water-quality"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-90","title":"Regenerative Beef Certification and Premium Market Development","prompt":"You are a regenerative agriculture consultant and marketing specialist advising a 5,200-acre cow-calf and stocker operation in Comanche County, Oklahoma (Post Oak Savanna, Stephenville fine sandy loam, 34-inch annual precipitation, 520 cow-calf pairs on improved bermudagrass and native range) that wants to achieve Land to Market Savory Institute EOV (Ecological Outcome Verification) certification and market grass-finished steers at a 35% premium. Design the certification and marketing program: (1) EOV monitoring program — establish 30 monitoring points across 3 landscape types (upland prairie, riparian corridor, hay meadow); annual Savory EOV monitoring: Robel pole visual obstruction readings, Parker Loop plant community transects, bare soil percentage, litter cover percentage, and soil aggregate stability; baseline bare ground 18%, litter cover 28%; targets: bare ground <10%, litter cover >45% by Year 5; (2) grazing management documentation — implement Savory Institute Holistic Planned Grazing (HPG) using annual grazing plan with 12-paddock rotation; photograph each paddock pre- and post-graze; record stocking density (AU/acre), rest periods, and rainfall; submit annual monitoring data to Land to Market platform for third-party verification; EOV certification cost: $2,800 initial + $1,400/year renewal; (3) grass-finishing program — retain 80 steers/year through 24-month grass-finish on native range; finish weight 1,100 lb live at $1.42/lb conventional versus $1.95/lb regenerative premium; processing at USDA-inspected facility at $380/head; Land to Market certified beef commands 40% premium at Whole Foods Market and online direct-to-consumer; (4) Whole Foods Market supplier qualification — Whole Foods requires: third-party animal welfare audit (Global Animal Partnership Step 4 certification, $1,200/audit), antibiotic-free and hormone-free documentation, Land to Market or Ecological Outcome Verified certification, minimum 100-head/year supply commitment; (5) direct-to-consumer expansion — build Shopify online store for quarter/half/whole grass-finished beef shipped in Styrofoam coolers with dry ice via FedEx 2-day; target 30 whole beefs/year × $3,800/whole = $114,000 direct revenue versus $77,000 conventional packer revenue on same animals. Produce an EOV monitoring schedule and market channel revenue comparison.","tags":["regenerative","grass-fed-beef","Oklahoma","EOV-certification","direct-marketing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-91","title":"Tilapia Recirculating Aquaculture System for Indoor Production","prompt":"You are an aquaculture engineer advising an entrepreneur group building a 6,000-square-foot indoor recirculating aquaculture system (RAS) in a former industrial building in Columbus, Ohio, targeting Nile tilapia (Oreochromis niloticus) production for sale to ethnic grocery retailers in the Columbus metropolitan area at $4.80/lb live weight. Design the complete RAS: (1) tank system design — four 10,000-gallon circular polyethylene production tanks (12-foot diameter, 4-foot depth); one 2,500-gallon fingerling nursery tank; biomedia filter (Kaldnes K5 at 500 liters/tank) for nitrification; mechanical filter drum (60-micron self-cleaning); UV sterilization (75 mJ/cm² at system flow rate); oxygen injection via Mazzei venturi injector maintaining DO >7.0 ppm; total water volume 42,500 gallons; (2) stocking and production cycle — stock production tanks at 0.5 lb/gallon = 5,000 lb standing biomass per tank; fingerlings from tilapia hatchery at $0.18/fingerling (0.3g, 3,000 per tank); grow to 1.5 lb harvest size in 7 months at 70°F water; FCR 1.4:1 with Zeigler Aquaculture 32% protein floating pellet; 4 tanks × 5,000 lb/harvest × 2 harvests/year = 40,000 lb/year production; (3) water chemistry management — pH maintained 7.0–7.5 using sodium bicarbonate (add 1 lb per 100 gallons water when pH drops below 7.0); ammonia TAN < 2.0 ppm; nitrite < 1.0 ppm; nitrate < 150 ppm (water exchange 5% daily); water temperature 72°F maintained by electric resistance heater at $4,200/month heating cost (calculated for Ohio winter); (4) NPDES discharge compliance — Ohio EPA General Permit OHG600000 for aquaculture discharges >1 MGD; daily discharge from water exchange: 2,100 gallons/day (5% × 42,500 gallons) = 0.76 MGD below permit threshold; nutrient-rich discharge diverted to adjacent greenhouse for hydroponic lettuce irrigation (1,200 sq ft) offsetting $8,400/year in water costs; (5) financial model — capital $580,000 (tanks $120,000, filtration $180,000, building modifications $220,000, startup $60,000); annual operating $210,000; revenue 40,000 lb × $4.80 = $192,000; Year 3 breakeven at expanded 60,000 lb with additional tank. Produce a water quality management log and 5-year financial projection.","tags":["aquaculture","tilapia","RAS","Columbus","indoor-production"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-92","title":"Pasture Renovation and Native Grass Restoration for Degraded Hay Ground","prompt":"You are a forage specialist and rangeland agronomist advising a 340-acre beef and hay operation in Blaine County, Oklahoma (Pratt loamy fine sand, pH 6.4, OM 1.4%, currently in bermudagrass-fescue mix degraded by drought and overgrazing, producing 1.8 tons DM/acre versus potential 3.2 tons) that wants to renovate 120 acres of hay ground using native warm-season grasses and improve the remaining 220 acres of bermudagrass pasture. Design the renovation program: (1) site assessment and species selection — conduct species composition transect (25-meter Parker Loop) on all fields; classify fields by weed pressure (johnsongrass, sandbur, sericea lespedeza presence); select native grass mix for 120 acres: big bluestem (Kaw variety, 4 lb PLS/acre) + little bluestem (Cimarron, 2 lb PLS/acre) + indiangrass (Osage, 2 lb PLS/acre) + sideoats grama (El Reno, 1 lb PLS/acre); source seed from Oklahoma Certified Seed dealers; (2) seedbed preparation — terminate bermudagrass 6 months before planting using glyphosate 32 oz/acre twice in summer growing season; fall burn of existing residue; spring disk to 3-inch depth; cultipacker firming before no-till drill seeding; for high-johnsongrass fields add pre-emergent pendimethalin (Prowl H₂O at 2 qt/acre) to suppress competition; (3) native grass establishment calendar — plant in May 1–15 window at soil temperature 65°F; no-till drill at 0.5-inch depth (Truax FLEX-II drill) in 7-inch rows; do not graze or cut first year; mow to 12-inch height if weeds exceed 24 inches in summer Year 1; Year 2 first grazing at 18-inch height with 10-day limit to promote tiller development; full production Year 3; (4) bermudagrass pasture improvement — aerate 220 acres in September with Aerway slit-tine aerator; overseed with Midland bermudagrass sprigs at 20 bushels/acre on prepared seedbed; apply 80 lb N/acre as ammonium nitrate in two split applications (April and June) to boost bermudagrass vigor against weed competition; (5) economic comparison — degraded hay ground at 1.8 tons × $85/ton = $153/acre; native grass hay at 2.8 tons × $95/ton premium = $266/acre; improvement cost $680/acre establishment; payback 5.4 years at $113/acre differential. Produce a species selection guide and establishment calendar.","tags":["forage","native-grass","Oklahoma","pasture-renovation","hay-production"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-93","title":"Precision Soil Sampling Network for Long-Term Nutrient Trend Monitoring","prompt":"You are a precision agriculture soil scientist advising a 2,800-acre corn-soybean operation in McLean County, Illinois (Flanagan-Drummer silty clay loam complex, pH 6.4–7.1, Bray P1 ranging 18–68 ppm, K 112–284 ppm) establishing a permanent soil sampling network to detect long-term nutrient trends rather than field-level averages. Design the monitoring program: (1) permanent sampling point design — establish 85 GPS-referenced permanent sampling points at 0.5-acre grids in representative management zones; mark each point with a 6-inch stainless steel T-post at 2-foot depth to enable exact relocation; collect 0–6 inch and 6–24 inch depth samples separately to track nutrient stratification under no-till; label with permanent PVC stake with point ID; (2) analytical protocol — submit to Midwest Laboratories (Omaha, NE) for complete analysis: pH, buffer pH, Bray P1 (acid soils) and Mehlich P, K, Mg, Ca, CEC, base saturation, OM, Zn, Mn, Cu, Fe, S-sulfate, B; cost $42/sample; 170 samples/year = $7,140/year; retain archived samples from each sampling date at −20°F for retrospective isotope analysis; (3) spatial trend analysis — compare 5-year moving average of Bray P1 by management zone; identify fields with declining P below 25 ppm Bray triggering switch from maintenance to build rates; identify P-excessive fields (>75 ppm) where P application should be suspended; overlay with yield monitor data to calculate P removal by crop in lb P₂O₅/acre/year; (4) nutrient budget construction — annual P budget per field: P applied (fertilizer + manure) minus P removed (yield × 0.35 lb P₂O₅/bu corn) = net P balance; target near-zero or slight-positive balance for long-term sustainability; fields with chronic positive balance (+ 20 lb P₂O₅/acre) trending toward 90-ppm threshold; (5) reporting and management decisions — generate annual nutrient trend report by field with 5-year trajectory graphs; present to farm operator in February planning meeting; adjust fertilizer recommendation to match removal rate × 1.05 on fields above build threshold. Produce a permanent sampling network layout map and annual nutrient budget spreadsheet template.","tags":["precision-ag","soil-sampling","nutrient-management","Illinois","long-term-monitoring"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-94","title":"Beginning Farmer Business Plan for Vegetable Market Garden Startup","prompt":"You are an agricultural economist and business planning consultant helping a beginning farmer in Story County, Iowa launching a 3-acre certified organic market garden on leased land (5-acre parcel, $420/year lease, Nicollet loam, pH 6.8, OM 3.4%) with $28,000 in startup capital and a target of reaching profitability by Year 2. [Describe your farm situation and current challenges] Design the comprehensive business plan: (1) enterprise selection and revenue planning — Year 1 crops: 1.5 acres salad mix and specialty lettuce ($18,400/acre gross), 0.5 acres heirloom tomatoes ($9,200/acre gross), 0.5 acres herbs and cut flowers ($12,800/acre gross), 0.5 acres root vegetables ($6,200/acre gross); Year 1 gross revenue target: $29,400; add 20-member CSA at $580/share = $11,600; total Year 1 revenue target: $41,000; (2) capital budget — irrigation system: $3,800 (Drip Depot drip tape + mainline + timer); high tunnel 14×96 foot: $6,200 (FarmTek Gothic Quonset); small tractor walk-behind (BCS 739 with tiller attachment): $4,800; hand tools, trays, seeding equipment: $2,400; certificationCCOF organic: $1,800; total capital expenditure $19,000; retain $9,000 operating reserve for seed, transplants, amendments; (3) production and marketing calendar — January–February: order seeds, start onions and leeks indoors; March: first tunnel plantings (spinach, arugula, salad mix); April: direct outdoor seedings, transplant tomatoes to tunnel; May–September: weekly CSA distributions and farmers market (Iowa State Farmers Market, Saturday, $28/stall); October: fall tunnel crops, garlic planting; November: garlic and root vegetable sales; (4) Year 1 operating cost breakdown — seed $2,400, amendments (composted chicken litter, rock phosphate) $1,800, CCOF certification $1,800, market fees $728, packaging $420, labor (self + 1 part-time at $16/hr × 400 hrs) $6,400; total Year 1 operating costs $13,548; Year 1 projected net: $41,000 − $13,548 = $27,452 gross contribution before owner draw; (5) 3-year growth plan — Year 2: expand CSA to 45 members ($26,100) + market sales; add food hub wholesale account (Iowa Food Hub, Ames) at 30% margin; Year 3: achieve $95,000 gross with $48,000 net owner draw. Produce a month-by-month cash flow projection and seed order planning guide.","tags":["farm-business","beginning-farmer","Iowa","organic","market-garden"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-95","title":"Precision Liming Program Using pH Variability Maps for Reduced Input Cost","prompt":"You are a precision agriculture specialist advising a 720-acre corn-soybean operation in Auglaize County, Ohio (Blount-Pewamo silty clay loam, pH ranging from 5.8 to 7.4 within single fields due to legacy lime misapplication, calcareous subsoil intrusions, and past manure applications) where uniform lime applications are causing overliming on 30% of acres and underliming on 25% of acres. Design a variable-rate liming program: (1) grid sampling design — collect soil pH and buffer pH (Shoemaker-McLean-Pratt buffer method) at 2.5-acre grids, 0–6 inch depth; 720 acres ÷ 2.5 = 288 grid samples analyzed by Midwest Laboratories at $8.50/sample including pH, buffer pH, and OM; total sampling cost $2,448; (2) pH map interpretation — use Kriging spatial interpolation in SSToolbox to generate continuous pH surface map; identify low-pH zones (pH <6.1) needing lime, high-pH zones (pH >7.2 with free carbonates — no lime needed), and optimal zones (pH 6.2–7.0 — maintenance lime only); (3) VRT lime prescription — lime rate by zone using Ohio recommendation: pH 5.8 buffer pH 6.4 = 2.8 tons ECCE 100 lime/acre; pH 6.1 buffer pH 6.7 = 1.6 tons/acre; pH 7.0+ = 0 tons/acre; upload prescription to lime tender controller (Loral VRT spread controller on 12-ton tender); (4) economic analysis — uniform rate program: 720 acres × 2.0 tons/acre = 1,440 tons × $38/ton spread = $54,720; VRT prescription: 400 acres at average 2.2 tons + 200 acres at 1.0 tons + 120 acres at 0 tons = 1,080 tons × $38/ton = $41,040; VRT savings $13,680 versus $2,448 sampling cost = net $11,232 savings per 3-year liming cycle; (5) follow-up verification — re-sample high-variability zones every 2 years versus 3-year cycle for stable zones; integrate pH maps with yield monitor data to verify pH-yield correlation (R² target >0.65 in pH-limiting zones). Produce a VRT lime prescription workflow and economic comparison table.","tags":["precision-ag","liming","pH-variability","Ohio","VRT"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-96","title":"Farmworker Safety and Heat Illness Prevention Program","prompt":"You are an occupational health specialist and agricultural HR consultant advising a 480-acre fresh vegetable operation in Imperial County, California (summer temperatures regularly 110–115°F from June through September) employing 45 H-2A visa workers and 12 permanent employees harvesting romaine lettuce, cantaloupe, and green onions under multi-year contracts with Fresh Del Monte and Dole. Design a comprehensive heat illness prevention and worker safety program: (1) California OSHA Heat Illness Prevention Standard compliance (Title 8 CCR 3395) — provide 1 quart cool water per worker per hour during heat illness risk periods (above 80°F); shade structures required when temperature exceeds 80°F — install 4 × 20×40-foot shade canopies on field edges with 30-minute maximum walk to shade from any work area; (2) high heat procedures above 95°F — implement mandatory 10-minute preventive cool-down rest periods every 2 hours; assign a trained heat illness prevention officer per field crew to monitor workers for early symptoms (excessive sweating cessation, confusion, hot dry skin = heat stroke emergency); (3) acclimatization protocol — new workers performing strenuous outdoor work must be acclimatized; California regulation requires close observation during days 1–14; limit new worker hours to 50% of experienced worker pace on day 1, increasing 10% per day; maintain acclimatization log; (4) emergency response plan — identify nearest emergency facility (Pioneers Memorial Hospital, Brawley, 8 miles) and maintain updated employee emergency contacts; field supervisors carry charged cell phones; conduct 2 heat illness response drills per season; ice-water immersion kit (80-quart cooler) in each crew van for heat stroke first response; (5) H-2A housing compliance — verify evaporative cooling system in H-2A housing maintains sleeping area temperature at or below 87°F; install indoor Onset HOBO temperature loggers and maintain log for Cal/OSHA inspection; California regulation requires documentation of nighttime temperatures for inspectors. Produce a daily heat safety monitoring log and emergency response protocol.","tags":["farm-labor","worker-safety","heat-illness","California","H-2A"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-97","title":"Cover Crop Seed Production Enterprise for On-Farm Revenue Diversification","prompt":"You are an agronomist and seed systems specialist advising a 560-acre diversified grain operation in Washington County, Iowa (Muscatine silty clay loam, OM 3.6%, existing seed cleaner building with Carter Day clipper) that wants to produce certified cover crop seed — cereal rye and hairy vetch — as a value-added enterprise capturing the $8–14/bu cereal rye seed premium over grain prices. Design the seed production program: (1) variety selection and certification — contact Iowa Crop Improvement Association for cereal rye variety certification; eligible varieties Maton, Hancock, and Elbon for Iowa-certified status; hairy vetch variety Veselka or Blackhawk certified through ICIA; obtain Foundation seed from ICIA seed inventory at $28/bu cereal rye; establish 80 acres cereal rye seed production and 40 acres hairy vetch; (2) production management for seed — cereal rye seed production fields require minimum 900-foot isolation from other cereal rye (cross-pollination risk); flag all seed fields with GPS; apply 80 lb N/acre pre-plant + 40 lb N/acre spring topdress for head development; harvest at 25–28% moisture with combine thresher settings modified for gentler cylinder speed (500 RPM) to prevent seed cracking; (3) seed cleaning and conditioning — process harvested seed through Carter Day clipper cleaner to remove inert matter, weed seed (dock, mustard), and cracked seed; target germination 85%+ for ICIA certification; bag in 50-lb bags with ICIA blue tag; store at 50°F and 50% RH in climate-controlled seed room; (4) market development — contact 5 cover crop seed distributors (Albert Lea Seed, Green Cover Seed, Millborn Seeds) for purchase agreements; target price $9.50/bu cereal rye seed versus $4.20/bu grain = $5.30/bu premium; 80 acres × 45 bu/acre yield × $9.50 = $34,200 versus $15,120 as grain = $19,080 improvement; (5) certification and regulatory costs — ICIA field inspection $280 per variety lot; lab germination and purity testing $85 per lot; ICIA licensing fee $0.05/bu marketed = total program cost $2,400/year for 2 crops. Produce a seed production calendar and certification compliance checklist.","tags":["farm-business","seed-production","cover-crops","Iowa","certification"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-98","title":"Riparian Buffer and Constructed Wetland for Agricultural Water Quality","prompt":"You are an agroecologist and conservation planner advising a 1,200-acre corn-soybean watershed in Hardin County, Ohio (Blount silty clay loam, multiple tile outlets discharging to Blanchard River — 303(d) listed impaired for nitrate and turbidity) on implementing edge-of-field water quality practices under the Ohio H2Ohio Program. Design a comprehensive buffer and wetland system: (1) riparian buffer establishment — identify 4,200 linear feet of stream bank requiring 35-foot buffer minimum; establish multi-species buffer using NRCS Practice 391: inner 10 feet native grasses (big bluestem, indiangrass, switchgrass at 8 lb PLS/acre), middle 15 feet native shrubs (silky dogwood, buttonbush, elderberry at 4-foot spacing), outer 10 feet trees (silver maple, cottonwood, bur oak at 8-foot spacing); (2) constructed wetland design — identify 4-acre low point receiving tile drainage from 280 upland acres; design surface flow constructed wetland (NRCS Practice 656) at 1 acre per 100 drainage acres = 2.8 acres minimum; berm at 3:1 side slope with clay core, 3-foot freeboard, outflow Agri Drain W-Series at 18-inch control setting; (3) nitrate removal performance — constructed wetland removes 30–55% of influent nitrate via denitrification; tile drainage NO₃-N 12 mg/L at 4.5 inches drainage = 27 lb N/acre/year from 280 acres = 7,560 lb N/year; 40% removal = 3,024 lb N/year reduction toward Ohio Maumee watershed target; (4) H2Ohio payment calculation — riparian buffer 4,200 ft × 35 ft = 3.4 acres × $340/acre practice payment = $1,156; constructed wetland 2.8 acres × $4,200/acre = $11,760; total H2Ohio payment $12,916 plus NRCS EQIP supplement at 50% project cost; (5) wildlife co-benefits — riparian buffer and wetland provides Wood Duck and Hooded Merganser nesting habitat; apply for USDA RCPP EQIP Migratory Bird Habitat Initiative at additional $38/acre management payment for 3 years. Produce a conservation practice layout map and 10-year cost-benefit analysis.","tags":["water-quality","riparian-buffer","Ohio","constructed-wetland","H2Ohio"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-99","title":"Farm Energy Audit and Grain Dryer Efficiency Upgrade Plan","prompt":"You are an agricultural energy efficiency specialist advising a 1,800-acre corn-soybean operation in Kossuth County, Iowa with a Sukup portable continuous flow grain dryer (24,000 BPH capacity) consuming 840,000 kWh/year of electricity and 38,000 gallons of LP gas at a combined energy cost of $112,400/year, representing 28% of total farm operating expenses. [Describe your farm situation and current challenges] Design the energy audit and upgrade plan: (1) dryer efficiency baseline measurement — install Grundfos submeter on dryer electrical service and separate LP flowmeter; measure energy consumption per bushel at varying harvest moisture inputs: record kWh/bu and BTU/bu at 24%, 22%, 20%, and 18% harvest moisture; compare to manufacturer spec of 2,500 BTU/lb water removed at optimal airflow; current efficiency estimate 3,100 BTU/lb (24% below optimal) indicating heat exchanger fouling or airflow restriction; (2) heat exchanger cleaning and maintenance — contract annual professional cleaning of propane burner and heat exchanger surfaces (fouled heat exchanger reduces efficiency 15–25%); replace burner orifices if combustion analysis shows CO > 150 ppm or combustion efficiency < 82%; install Dickey-john GAC 2500 grain moisture sampler for accurate harvest moisture monitoring to avoid over-drying (target 14.5% corn storage, not 13.5% = 1 point over-drying wastes 53,000 BTU/hour); (3) variable frequency drive (VFD) installation — install Toshiba G9 VFD on 75 HP main fan motor ($3,200 installed); reduce fan speed from 60 Hz to 52 Hz when ambient temperature above 65°F — fan affinity law: speed reduction 13% reduces power use 35%; estimated fan energy savings $8,400/year; 2-year payback; (4) propane price risk management — forward contract 50% of annual LP requirement (19,000 gallons) by July 1 at pre-harvest pricing; hedging with Iowa Propane Exchange protects against fall surge; remaining 50% purchased at spot market; historical analysis shows forward contract saves $0.18/gallon on average = $3,420/year; (5) USDA REAP energy audit grant — USDA REAP pays 25% of energy audit cost (AgSTAR audit $3,200 = $800 REAP grant); if audit identifies >25% efficiency improvement, REAP grant covers 25% of improvement project cost up to $500,000. Produce an energy baseline measurement form and dryer efficiency improvement ROI analysis.","tags":["farm-energy","grain-dryer","Iowa","efficiency","REAP"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"agr-100","title":"Farm-Level Carbon Inventory and Net-Zero Action Plan","prompt":"You are a sustainability specialist and agronomist advising a 1,500-acre corn-soybean-hog operation in Shelby County, Iowa (Monona silt loam, 2,400 finishing hogs on contract with Iowa Select Farms) that wants to conduct its first farm-level greenhouse gas inventory and develop a roadmap toward carbon neutrality by 2035 as part of a Walmart Project Gigaton supply chain commitment. Design the GHG inventory and net-zero roadmap: (1) GHG inventory methodology — use USDA COMET-Farm tool supplemented by IPCC Tier 2 emission factors; Scope 1 direct emissions: N₂O from synthetic fertilizer (195 lb N/acre × 840 corn acres × 0.01 emission factor × 44/28 N₂O conversion = 42 Mg CO₂e), CH₄ from 2,400 hog lagoon (46 Mg CO₂e per 1,000 hog spaces × 2.4 = 110 Mg CO₂e), diesel combustion (18,500 gallons × 0.0102 Mg CO₂e/gal = 189 Mg CO₂e); total estimated 341 Mg CO₂e Scope 1 emissions; (2) carbon sequestration baseline — current practices sequester approximately 85 Mg CO₂e/year; net farm footprint approximately +256 Mg CO₂e/year; COMET-Farm model inputs: field-level SOC at 0-30cm using NLCD land cover layer and 5-year history from FSA-578 form; (3) emission reduction pathway to 2030 — implement no-till on all 1,500 acres (+112 Mg CO₂e sequestration); cover crops cereal rye + legume blend (+65 Mg CO₂e on 840 corn acres); reduce synthetic N rate 15% through enhanced efficiency fertilizers DMPP-treated urea (−28 Mg CO₂e); covered lagoon biogas capture on hog unit (−88 Mg CO₂e); total projected footprint Year 5: +256 − 293 = −37 Mg CO₂e net negative; (4) Walmart Project Gigaton reporting — register farm in Walmart Sustainability Hub; report annual practice changes using Field to Market Fieldprint Calculator metrics; submit annual third-party verified GHG report using SCS Global Services ISO 14064-3 verification standard; target Fieldprint Water improvement from index 48 to 38 (20% reduction); (5) carbon revenue — Growers Edge carbon program payment on verified reductions: 293 Mg CO₂e × $24/tonne × 80% after buffer pool = $5,626/year; additional Walmart supplier sustainability incentive payment at 0.25% price premium on $2.1M commodity sales = $5,250/year. Produce a 10-year GHG tracking spreadsheet and net-zero implementation timeline.","tags":["farm-business","GHG-inventory","net-zero","Iowa","sustainability"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true}
];
